window.KB_BODY[70]={"tsb220008":"<h2>New Exhaust Muffler Bracket</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QSK60 CM2150 MCRS</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><p>A new exhaust muffler bracket with a cutout for increased clearance for turbocharger oil drain line is now available.</p><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><p>There is interference between the turbocharger support bracket and the turbocharger oil drain line. This contact can lead to fretting damage to the turbocharger oil drain line tubing.</p><p><b>Service Instructions</b></p><ul><li>Order existing or new part number to resolve clearance issues. All existing part number stock has been re-worked to match new part number and can still be used.</li><li>If installing the new exhaust muffler bracket and a thicker spacer has been added to the turbocharger oil drain line at the turbocharger connecting joint, this can be removed and restored to the original factory design using correct cap screws and new gaskets.</li></ul><p><b>Service Parts Availability</b></p><p>Service parts are available. See Table 1 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Turbocharger Support Bracket</td><td>3641080</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>5542739</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Identification</b></p><p>New part can be identified by part number stamping on the component and by the cutout as shown in Figure 2 below.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/11r00570.png\" alt=\"11r00570.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Existing Part Number 3641080.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/11r00571.png\" alt=\"11r00571.png\"></figure><p>Figure 2, New Part Number 5542739.</p><p><b>Part Inventory Action</b></p><p>Use existing part number inventory before using the new part number.</p><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 2.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN Last</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>33226335</td><td>5 October 2021</td><td>Daventry</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb220016":"<h2>Leaking Coolant From Turbocharger Coolant Return Line</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QSK60 CM2150 MCRS</li></ul><p><b>Issue Summary</b></p><p>Symptom:</p><ul><li>Coolant leak</li></ul><p>Root Cause:</p><ul><li>Incorrect hose clamp sizing</li></ul><p><b>Verification</b></p><p>If a coolant leak from turbo coolant plumbing return line, Part Number 5375486, is observed, follow instructions in resolution section for fix.</p><p><b>Resolution</b></p><ul><li>If a coolant leak from turbo coolant plumbing return line, Part Number 5375486, has been observed replace the coolant plumbing hose with a new one, Part Number 5375486, and secure hose with quantity three of clip, Part Number 3649927.</li><li>For proper removal and install instructions, see QSK60 CM2150 MCRS, Bulletin <a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/4021530\">4021530</a>. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/56-010-041\">Refer to Procedure 010-041</a> in Section 10.</li></ul><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><p>Original clips did <b>not</b> have proper fit which allowed fretting between turbocharger coolant hose and clamps resulting in coolant leak.</p><p><b>Service Parts Availability</b></p><p>Service parts are available. See Table 1 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>CLIP</td><td>3657725</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>3649927</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 2.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>33226938</td><td>13 December 2021</td><td>Daventry Engine Plant</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb220018":"<h2>New Eliminator™ Lubricating Oil Filter Assembly</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>KV38 All</li><li>KV50 All</li><li>QSK38 All</li><li>QSK45 All</li><li>QSK50 All</li><li>QSK60 All</li><li>QSK78 All</li><li>QSK95 All</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><p>New Eliminator™ Assemblies are being released with revised internals such as an improved hydraulic motor, centrifuge, and new filtering element screens which have a finer mesh screen to improve filtering capability.</p><p><b>Service Instructions</b></p><ul><li>The filter element screens are no longer purchased in pairs, all elements must now be replaced as a full assembly.</li><li>The cleaning and inspecting procedure for the filter element screens is now <b>only</b> recommended in the case of a malfunction event or if in service cleaning is required.</li><li>If damage is found to any of the existing element screens the recommended direction is to replace all filter elements with the new part number, although alternatively the damaged elements could be replaced with the existing part number. The new filter elements is <b>not</b> be mixed in with the existing part numbers.</li></ul><p><b>Service Parts Availability</b></p><p>Service parts are available. See Tables 1 and 2 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts New Eliminator™ Assembly</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Lubricating Oil Filter (QSK78 CM500)</td><td>3649467/5538155</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>5540795</td></tr><tr><td>Lubricating Oil Filter (QSK78 CM2350)</td><td>5538359</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>5540795</td></tr><tr><td>Lubricating Oil Filter (QSK60)</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5372017\">5372017</a></td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5540796\">5540796</a></td></tr><tr><td>Lubricating Oil Filter (QSK60 CM2350)</td><td>5372167</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5540796\">5540796</a></td></tr><tr><td>Lubricating Oil Filter (QSK45)</td><td>5371886</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>5540797</td></tr><tr><td>Lubricating Oil Filter (QSK38/50)</td><td>5538131</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>5540798</td></tr><tr><td>With Filling Tube</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Lubricating Oil Filter (QSK38/50 CM2350) With Filling Tube</td><td>5538135</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>5540798</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Service Parts New Eliminator™ Element Screens</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Oil Filter Element</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3393557\">3393557</a></td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>3651716</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Compatibility</b></p><p>New filter element screens are <b>not</b> to be mixed in with the existing part numbers, if new and existing screens are used together this can cause damage to the internals.</p><p><b>Part Identification</b></p><p>The filter element screens are only distinguishable by the pressed supplier part number 1800-03335 for the new screen and 1800-03336 on the old screens.</p><p><b>Part Inventory Action</b></p><p>Use existing part number inventory before using the new part number.</p><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 3.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 3, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td><td>Shop Order</td></tr><tr><td>66305764 (QSK78)</td><td>24 April 2020</td><td>Daventry</td><td>SO11656</td></tr><tr><td>33221666 (QSK60)</td><td>19 May 2020</td><td>Daventry</td><td>SO10798</td></tr><tr><td>33221557 (QSK60 CM2350)</td><td>27 April 2020</td><td>Daventry</td><td>SO11807</td></tr><tr><td>ESN Last</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td><td>Shop Order</td></tr><tr><td>33221517 (QSK45)</td><td>17 April 2020</td><td>Daventry</td><td>SO11947</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on the engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb220022":"<h2>Engine Control Module (ECM) Will Not Communicate or Will Not Accept ECM Calibration Code</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>ROM Booted CM2180 ECM should <b>not</b> attempt to be recovered by recalibrating. See Technical Service Bulletin, INSITE™ Electronic Service Tool Error 5080 - Electronic Control Module (ECM) ROM-Booted or ROM-Booted in a Password Protected State, TSB150120.</p></div></div><p>All models with Engine Control Modules (ECM) except CM420 (B5.9G, B5.9LPG, C8.3G, L10G), ECM B (CELECT™), CM400 (CENTRY™), CM800 (ISBe, ISB four-cylinder), and CM551D (ISB light-duty Chrysler™).</p><p><b>Applicable Engine Control Modules:</b></p><ul><li>CM2150</li><li>CM2180/CM2380</li><li>CM2220</li><li>CM2250</li><li>CM2330</li><li>CM2350/CM2450</li><li>CM2450</li><li>CM2358A</li><li>CM2620</li><li>CM2670</li><li>CM2880</li><li>CM3230</li><li>CM500</li><li>CM550</li><li>CM552</li><li>CM554</li><li>CM556B</li><li>CM558</li><li>CM570</li><li>CM700</li><li>CM850</li><li>CM870</li><li>CM871</li><li>CM875</li><li>CM876</li><li>ECM C</li></ul><p><b>Issue Summary</b></p><p>Some ECMs have been replaced and Cummins Inc. has determined a portion of them were operating properly after being reset and calibrated. Unnecessary replacements of functional ECMs can be avoided by following the ROM boot and calibration procedures listed in this document.</p><p>Symptom:</p><ul><li>No communication with ECM</li><li>ECM does <b>not</b> accept ECM calibration code</li></ul><p>Root Cause:</p><ul><li>ECM calibration was <b>not</b> performed correctly</li><li>Power or communication interruption during ECM calibration</li><li>Incorrect ECM calibration code</li></ul><p><b>Verification</b></p><p>Verify there is no communication with the ECM or that the ECM will <b>not</b> accept a calibration code.</p><p><b>Resolution</b></p><p>Before replacing the ECM, reset the ECM using the ROM boot process and attempt to recalibrate the ECM.</p><ul><li>Reset the ECM software using the ROM boot procedures listed in Table 1 and verify that INSITE™ shows a similar message as in Figure 1 below.</li><li>Recalibrate the ECM using the ECM calibration procedures listed in Table 1 below.</li><li>For general tool information, including the correct installation configuration and required cables, see the ECM-specific Bench Calibration Base Harness Service Tool Instruction, Bulletin 3377791.</li><li>If the bench calibration base harness or any of the required hardware is <b>not</b> available, follow the technical escalation process.</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/19r99820.png\" alt=\"19r99820.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Example of the Error Message From INSITE™ Confirming the ECM Is Rom-Booted and Needs Recalibration.</p><p><b>Associated Publications</b></p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, ECM Associated Publications</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Publication Type</td><td>Bulletin Number</td><td>Procedure Title</td><td>Procedure</td><td>Section</td></tr><tr><td>ECM ROM Boot</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>See corresponding Service Manual</td><td>Engine Control Module ROM Boot</td><td>019-427</td><td>19</td></tr><tr><td>Electronic Control System Troubleshooting and Repair Manual</td><td>See corresponding Electronic Control System Troubleshooting and Repair Manual</td><td>Engine Control Module ROM Boot</td><td>019-427</td><td>19</td></tr><tr><td>Technical Service Bulletin</td><td>TSB150120</td><td>INSITE™ Electronic Service Tool Error 5080 - Electronic Control Module (ECM) ROM-Booted or ROM-Booted in a Password Protected State</td><td>-</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>ECM Calibration</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Service Tool Instruction</td><td>3377791</td><td>ECM Bench Calibration Base Harness</td><td>-</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>See corresponding Service Manual</td><td>Engine Control Module Calibration Code</td><td>019-032</td><td>19</td></tr><tr><td>Electronic Control System Troubleshooting and Repair Manual</td><td>See corresponding Electronic Control System Troubleshooting and Repair Manual</td><td>Engine Control Module Calibration Code</td><td>019-032</td><td>19</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb220058":"<h2>Valve Guide Wear Caused by Oil and Carbon Buildup from Valve Stem Seal: Black Smoke, Engine Noise, or Lower Power</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QST30 CM552</li><li>QST30 PG CM850</li></ul><p><b>Issue Summary</b></p><p>Symptoms:</p><ul><li>Excessive black smoke</li><li>Abnormal engine noise</li><li>Low power</li></ul><p>Root Cause:</p><ul><li>Existing Tier 2 valve stem seal is <b>not</b> intended for the engine operating exhaust port pressures and hence, does <b>not</b> seal the exhaust gases or meter oil per requirement.</li></ul><p><b>Verification</b></p><ul><li>Upon inspection would oil/soot mixture found packed in the cylinder head near valve stems and springs and valve stem seals.</li><li>Upon removal of the valve stem guides and valves severe amount of corrosion, carbon deposits and material loss from valve guides is observed.</li></ul><p><b>Resolution</b></p><p>Replace Tier 2 Valve Stem Seal with Tier 4 Valve Stem Seal and/or Replace entire cylinder head assembly with new part number.</p><ol><li>Remove Suspect Cylinder Head from Engine</li></ol><ul><li>See corresponding Service Manual. Reference Procedure 002-004 in Section 2.</li></ul><ol><li>Suspect Cylinder Head Removal Preparation</li></ol><ul><li>Purchase a full set of QST30 cylinder heads. See Parts list in Appendix A1.1.</li><li>Purchase all new parts as specified in parts list and reference reuse list for reworking used good cylinder heads in Appendix A1.2</li></ul><ol><li>Disassemble Suspect Cylinder Head</li></ol><ul><li>Disassemble cylinder head following Procedure 002-004 and 002-020 in Section 2</li><li>Skip removing exhaust and intake valve guides unless reworking good used cylinder head.</li><li>Remove tier 2 valve stem oil seals.</li><li>Skip Clean, Inspect, Leak and Pressure Test sections in 002-004 in Section 2 unless reusing good used cylinder head continue following all procedures.</li></ul><ol><li>Installing Replacement Cylinder Head</li></ol><ul><li>Install replacement cylinder head. See corresponding Service Manual. Reference Procedure 002-004 in Section 2</li></ul><ol><li>Reworking Suspect Tier 2 Cylinder Head</li></ol><p>Assembly Preparation</p><p>Gather all supplies and tools needed for performing assembly.</p><p>Cummins® Tools Needed Prior to Assembly:</p><ul><li>Valve guide driver, Part Number 6304260</li><li>Valve seal driver, Part Number 3163429</li><li>Valve spring compressor, Part Number ST-448 or Part Number 3163639, or equivalent</li><li>Valve spring stand, Part Number ST-1022</li><li>Compressor plate, Part Number 3375043</li></ul><p>Assembly:</p><ul><li>First few cylinder heads assembled, measure all valve guide lengths, and record the value and compare with guide overall length dimensions located in Appendix B1.</li><li>Install exhaust and intake valve guides using tool when reworking good used cylinder head</li><li>Verify guide installs properly to 21.5 mm by measuring guide exposed difference (see B1.2).</li><li>Install Tier 4 valve stem seals on all exhaust and intake valve guides by using tool, Part Number 3163429.</li><li>Continue assembling cylinder head. See corresponding Service Manual. Reference Procedure 002-004 and 002-020 in Section 2.</li></ul><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><p>Cylinder head assembly was changed to counter recurring valve stem seal malfunctions in the field.</p><p><b>Service Parts Availability</b></p><p>Service parts are available. See Table 1 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Cylinder Head</td><td>40686731</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>55936202</td></tr><tr><td>1 All parts have already been installed with new (Tier 4) valve stem seal and valve stem guide pressed to proper depth. 2 New part number in the process of being released.</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Service Tools and Training Available</b></p><p>New tool for installing valve stem guide to the proper depth has been released and will be in stock shortly. See Appendix B1 for dimensional print.</p><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 2.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>37284774</td><td>1 June 2021</td><td>Seymour Engine Plant</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Appendix A1</b></p><p>A1.1 - New Cylinder Head Parts List</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 3, New Cylinder Head Parts List</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Item Number</td><td>Description</td><td>Part Number</td><td>Head Quantity</td><td>Minimum Purchase Quantity</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Tier (T) 2 Cylinder Head Assembly</td><td>5593620</td><td>1</td><td>&gt;12</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Rework Parts List</p><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Based on Condition denotes following clean, inspection, and leak test procedures found on Procedure 002-004 and 002-020 in Section 2.</p></div></div><p>Reference assembly layout continued in Appendix A2 for item number references.</p><p>A1.2 - Rework Parts List</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 4, Rework Parts List</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Item Number in Figure 1</td><td>Description</td><td>Part Number</td><td>Head Quantity</td><td>Reuse/Replace</td><td>Comments</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>T2 Cylinder Head Assembly</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4068673\">4068673</a></td><td>1</td><td>Reuse</td><td>Based on condition</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>T2 Cylinder Head Rebuild Kit</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5579210\">5579210</a></td><td>1</td><td>Purchase</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>T4 Valve Stem Seal</td><td>2897022</td><td>4</td><td>Purchase</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Valve Collets</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3092344\">3092344</a></td><td>8</td><td>Replace</td><td>Discard and use new collets</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Exhaust Valve Kit -1-Valve, 2-Collets</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3804867\">3804867</a></td><td>2</td><td>Replace -Valve stems and collets</td><td>Use new valve stems and collets</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Intake Valve Kit -1-Valve, 2-Collets</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3804866\">3804866</a></td><td>2</td><td>Replace -Valve stems and collets</td><td>Use new valve stems and collets</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>T2 Valve Stem Seal</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3092608\">3092608</a></td><td>4</td><td>Replace</td><td>Discard and use T4 seals</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Valve Stem Guide</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3092014\">3092014</a></td><td>4</td><td>Replace</td><td>Use new valve guides</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>Intake Valve Seat (Valve Insert)</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3094159\">3094159</a></td><td>2</td><td>Reuse</td><td>Based on condition</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Exhaust Valve Seat (Valve Insert)</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3092461\">3092461</a></td><td>2</td><td>Reuse</td><td>Based on condition</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Valve Spring Retainer</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3092510\">3092510</a></td><td>4</td><td>Reuse</td><td>Based on condition</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Large Spring</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3092508\">3092508</a></td><td>4</td><td>Reuse</td><td>Based on condition</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Small Spring</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3092509\">3092509</a></td><td>4</td><td>Reuse</td><td>Based on condition</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Valve Spring Guide</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3092511\">3092511</a></td><td>4</td><td>Reuse</td><td>Based on condition</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Appendix A2</b></p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/02r00141.png\" alt=\"02r00141.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, QST30 CM552 Cylinder Head Layout.</p><p><b>Appendix B1</b></p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/02r00142.png\" alt=\"02r00142.png\"></figure><p>Figure 2, Valve Guide OAL: 72.5 0/-0.5 mm.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/02r00143.png\" alt=\"02r00143.png\"></figure><p>Figure 3, Valve Guide Install Depth Difference.</p><p>Previous Valve Guide Install Depth Difference 23 mm +/-0.2 mm</p><p>New Valve Guide Install Depth Difference 21.5 mm +/-0.2 mm</p><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb220078":"<h2>New Fuel Pump with Revised Outlet Check Valve</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><p>Engine:</p><ul><li>QSK60 CM2150 MCRS</li></ul><p>Application and Rating:</p><ul><li>Construction engines with 2237Kw [ 3000 hp ]</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><p>A new outlet check valve design has been integrated into the fuel pump assembly to improve reliability for 3000 hp construction applications.</p><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><ul><li>To reduce fuel pump cavitation at the fuel pump outlet check valve and improve reliability, a new fuel pump part number has been released.</li><li>The new outlet check valve design has improved elongated slots in the check valve to resolve cavitation issues.</li></ul><p><b>Service Parts Availability</b></p><p>Service parts are available. See Table 1 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Fuel Pump</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5540246\">5540246</a></td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>5541849</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 2.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN Last</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>33227993</td><td>30 March 2022</td><td>Daventry</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on the engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb220121":"<h2>New Heat Shield Part Number</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QSK50 CM2150 MCRS</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><ul><li>Existing heat shields have been superseded with new designs of heat shields.</li><li>The new designs have driven part number changes that are communicated in Table 1 below.</li></ul><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><p>Improvements have been made on the new designs to increase thermal containment efficiency and reduce the space claim of the heat shields.</p><p><b>Service Instructions</b></p><p>New part numbers <b>must</b> be fitted as a pair. See Part Compatibility section below for more information.</p><p><b>Service Parts</b></p><p>Service parts are available. See Table 1 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Shield, Heat</td><td>3643681</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>5542292</td></tr><tr><td>Shield, Heat</td><td>3643682</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>5542293</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Compatibility</b></p><ul><li>Do <b>not</b> mix existing and new part numbers.</li><li>Example: 5542292 is designed to fit with 5542293 and will <b>not</b> fit with 3643682.</li><li>The new parts together give full backwards compatibility to the overall heat shield assembly.</li></ul><p><b>Part Inventory Action</b></p><p>Use existing part number inventory before using the new part number.</p><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 2.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>33224989</td><td>1 June 2021</td><td>Daventry</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on the engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb220129":"<h2>Cylinder Head Intake Valve Stem Seal Change</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QSK60 CM2150 MCRS</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><p>A new cylinder head part number has been released following a change of intake valve stem seal.</p><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><p>A new valve stem seal has been implemented on intake side to increase lubricating oil flow and reduce valve guide wear over the life of the cylinder head, preventing excessive guide wear requiring early cylinder head changes.</p><p><b>Service Parts Availability</b></p><p>Service parts are available. See Table 1 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Cylinder Head</td><td>5635345</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5694550\">5694550</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Inventory Action</b></p><p>Existing cylinder head part numbers have been built with new valve stem seal under deviation since August 2020 and the change in part number is purely for tracing purposes. Continue to use existing cylinder head part number until no longer available and superseded by new part number.</p><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 2.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First (built with new cylinder head, Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5694550\">5694550</a>)</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>33229561</td><td>26 August 2022</td><td>Daventry</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on the engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb220130":"<h2>Improved Exhaust Manifold</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QSK60 CM2150 MCRS</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><p>The exhaust manifold has received a casting change. The change improves the pouring process reducing the shrinkage porosity and core shift increasing component life. A change has also been introduced to the fillet radius connecting the main “trunk” to the cylinder head connecting branch. The radius of this area has been changed from 7 mm to 13 mm reducing the stress at this location.</p><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><p>Customers have been reporting exhaust gas leaks coming from the exhaust manifolds caused by cracks. A factory led engineering project identified a high stress location in the manifold at the radius connecting the main trunk to the cylinder head branch. This high stress caused a crack to initiate that was then accelerated through the component by porosity and core shift.</p><p><b>Service Parts Availability</b></p><p>The exhaust manifold part number has <b>not</b> changed. The part numbers affected are exhaust manifold, Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3642650\">3642650</a> and <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3642653\">3642653</a>.</p><p><b>Part Identification</b></p><p>Improved parts can be identified by the “B” stamping. See Figure 1 below.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/11r00592.png\" alt=\"11r00592.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Exhaust Manifold Identification Location.</p><p><b>Part Inventory Action</b></p><p>Use existing part number inventory before using the new part number.</p><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 1.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Part Number</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>33228620</td><td>3642650B</td><td>6 June 2022</td><td>Daventry</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on the engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb220145":"<h2>Remotely Mounted Fuel Pump Dedicated Lubricating Oil Filter</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QSK60 CM2150 MCRS</li><li>QSK60 CM850 MCRS</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><p>This remote mount dedicated lubricating oil filter has been released to allow customers to fit the fuel pump oil filter independent to the engine.</p><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><p>In some applications there is insufficient space to install the on engine dedicated lubricating oil filter. To enable units to benefit from extra filtration to the fuel pump and improved robustness, Cummins Inc. has released the off-engine version.</p><p><b>Customer Communication</b></p><p>This hardware can also incorporate the oil pressure improvement lines. See Technical Service Bulletin, New Fuel Pump Oil Supply Line: Flywheel Supplied, TSB220042 for flywheel housing supplied and for cylinder block supplied.</p><p><b>Service Instructions</b></p><p>This installation should <b>only</b> be used in applications where the on engine dedicated lubrication filter is <b>not</b> able be used because of space constraints.</p><p><b>Service Parts Availability</b></p><p>Service parts are available. See Table 1 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td><td>Quantity</td><td>Item Number in Figure 1</td></tr><tr><td>Clamp</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>3021331</td><td>2</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Male Union</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3171722\">3171722</a></td><td>1</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>Male Union</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3171818\">3171818</a></td><td>2</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>O-ring Seal</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3328463\">3328463</a></td><td>2</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>Male Elbow Union</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3331818\">3331818</a></td><td>1</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>Pressure Sensor</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>3408560</td><td>1</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>Cap Screw</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3900629\">3900629</a></td><td>2</td><td>11</td></tr><tr><td>Cap screw</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3900631\">3900631</a></td><td>1</td><td>13</td></tr><tr><td>Cap screw</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3900634\">3900634</a></td><td>3</td><td>14</td></tr><tr><td>Cap Screw</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3903112\">3903112</a></td><td>4</td><td>15</td></tr><tr><td>Cap Screw</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>3936643</td><td>1</td><td>16</td></tr><tr><td>Filter</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4323856\">4323856</a></td><td>1</td><td>18</td></tr><tr><td>Pressure Switch</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4924925\">4924925</a></td><td>1</td><td>19</td></tr><tr><td>Filter head</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>5538894</td><td>1</td><td>20</td></tr><tr><td>Filter Head</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>5538895</td><td>1</td><td>21</td></tr><tr><td>Wiring Harness</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>5540220</td><td>1</td><td>25</td></tr><tr><td>Bracket</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>5540221</td><td>1</td><td>26</td></tr><tr><td>Oil Tube</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>5635352</td><td>1</td><td>27</td></tr><tr><td>Oil Tube</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>5635354</td><td>1</td><td>28</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/07r00298.png\" alt=\"07r00298.png\"></figure><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 2.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>33227577</td><td>15 February 2022</td><td>Daventry</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb220146":"<h2>New Fuel Pump Oil Supply Line</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QSK60 CM2150 MCRS</li><li>QSK60 CM850 MCRS</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><p>A new oil supply line has been developed for the modular common rail system fuel pump. The new oil supply line increases the oil pressure available to the fuel pump and improves the reliability and component life.</p><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><p>There have been reports of fuel pump fault codes relating to low oil and fuel pressure. To improve reliability, a new oil supply line has been released that increases the pressure of oil available to the fuel pump by feeding oil directly from the main oil rifle to the fuel pump accessory drive.</p><p><b>Service Instructions</b></p><ul><li>Installation and removal instructions have been added to the Service Manual. See Publications Affected section below for details.</li><li>These parts are <b>only</b> compatible with engines manufactured after 02 October 2020 or are utilizing a new cylinder block. See Part Compatibility section below.</li></ul><p><b>Service Parts Availability</b></p><p>Service parts are available. See Table 1 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td><td>Quantity required</td><td>Figure 1 Item Number</td></tr><tr><td>Pressure Sensor</td><td>-</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>2872883</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Screw, Hexagon Head</td><td>-</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3087279\">3087279</a></td><td>2</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Screw, Hexagon Head</td><td>-</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3093923\">3093923</a></td><td>2</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>O-Ring Seal</td><td>-</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3328463\">3328463</a></td><td>2</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>Union</td><td>-</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3331873\">3331873</a></td><td>2</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>Retaining clamp</td><td>-</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3636638\">3636638</a></td><td>2</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>Tube Clamp</td><td>-</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3647803\">3647803</a></td><td>4</td><td>7</td></tr><tr><td>Threaded Plug</td><td>-</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3678921\">3678921</a></td><td>1</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>Screw, Hexagon Head</td><td>-</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3900630\">3900630</a></td><td>2</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>Screw, Hexagon Head</td><td>-</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3900632\">3900632</a></td><td>2</td><td>10</td></tr><tr><td>Screw, Hexagon Head</td><td>-</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3902460\">3902460</a></td><td>1</td><td>11</td></tr><tr><td>Screw. Hexagon Head</td><td>-</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3916069\">3916069</a></td><td>3</td><td>12</td></tr><tr><td>Dedicated Lubrication Filter</td><td>-</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4323856\">4323856</a></td><td>1</td><td>14</td></tr><tr><td>Oil Pressure Switch</td><td>-</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4924925\">4924925</a></td><td>1</td><td>15</td></tr><tr><td>Elbow Union</td><td>-</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4967227\">4967227</a></td><td>1</td><td>16</td></tr><tr><td>Lubricating Oil Tube</td><td>-</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5539844\">5539844</a></td><td>1</td><td>17</td></tr><tr><td>Mounting Bracket</td><td>-</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5539846\">5539846</a></td><td>2</td><td>18</td></tr><tr><td>Wiring Harness</td><td>-</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5542339\">5542339</a></td><td>1</td><td>19</td></tr><tr><td>Lubricating Filter Head</td><td>-</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5544805\">5544805</a></td><td>1</td><td>20</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/07r00297.png\" alt=\"07r00297.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, New Oil Supply Line Kit Parts Breakdown.</p><p><b>Part Compatibility</b></p><ul><li>The oil supply line kit is available on construction application engines built in Daventry. The oil supply line kit can <b>only</b> be installed on engines operating in the field if they are using a new cylinder block or were built after 02 October 2020. Following this date, a new cylinder block was introduced with the correct drilling utilized by the new oil pressure supply line. See Technical Service Bulletin, New Cylinder Block to Reduce MCRS Fuel Pump Malfunction, <a class=\"lnk tsb\" href=\"#/doc/tsb210009\">TSB210009</a>.</li><li>If the engine was built before 02 October 2020 and utilizes an existing cylinder block, there is an alternative supply line available that uses oil from the flywheel housing of the engine. See Technical Service Bulletin, New Fuel Pump Oil Supply Line: Flywheel Supplied, TSB220042 for details.</li></ul><p><b>Part Identification</b></p><p>The new cylinder block supplied oil supply line can be identified by the supply point being a main oil rifle connection. See Figure 2 below.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/07r00240.png\" alt=\"07r00240.png\"></figure><p>Figure 2, New Cylinder Block Supplied Oil Supply Line.</p><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 2.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>33226913</td><td>6 December 2021</td><td>Daventry</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Publications Affected</b></p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 3, Publications Affected</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Manual Type</td><td>Engine</td><td>Bulletin Number</td><td>Procedure Title</td><td>Procedure</td><td>Section</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QSK60 CM2150 MCRS</td><td><a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/4021530\">4021530</a></td><td>Fuel Pump Drive Lubricating Oil Supply Line</td><td><a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/56-007-112\">Refer to Procedure 007-112</a></td><td>7</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb220196":"<h2>Electric Starting Motor Changes</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>KV38 (All Versions)</li><li>KV50 (All Versions)</li><li>QSK38 (All Versions)</li><li>QSK50 (All Versions)</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><p>A new starting motor is available.</p><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><p>Production has ceased on all MS7 and M125 starting motor and the M128 starting motor is the replacement.</p><p><b>Service Instructions</b></p><p>Parts and Compatibility</p><ul><li>If using a single (quantity 1) MS7 or M125 starting motor on an engine. The M128 starting motor is a direct replacement.</li><li>If using dual (quantity 2) MS7 or M125 starting motor on an engine. Then both starting motors <b>must</b> be replaced with M128 starting motor.</li><li>If using triple (quantity 3) starting motor. All 3 starting motor <b>must</b> be replaced with M128 starting motor.</li><li>Table 3 shows starting motor part number supersession and all new part numbers.</li><li>New brackets have been released to mount the magnetic switches to the M128 starting motor and are listed in Table 3.</li></ul><p>Installation</p><ul><li>The orientation of the starting motor when fitting to the flywheel housing is dictated by the flat side of the mounting flange. The flat side of the mounting flange is the side that <b>must</b> be oriented to the side face of the cylinder block for correct starting motor orientation. Figure 1 shows the flat face of the starting motor mounting flange.</li><li>The orientation of the starting motor will also dictate the magnetic switch bracket to be used.</li><li>Figure 2 shows the correct mounting bracket depending on starting motor orientation.</li><li>Table 2 communicates the correct magnetic switch bracket part number.</li><li>Figure 3 shows the exploded starting motor assembly detailing the magnetic switch and is applicable for both orientations of mounting bracket and single/dual magnetic switch assemblies.</li><li>A torque table (Table 2) is available for updated joints on the assemblies.</li><li>Figure 4 shows the wiring terminals that <b>must</b> be used to wire the starting motor correctly. The T50C (Ignition ‘S&#x27;) terminal <b>must</b> be balanced using 16AWG wire between both T50C (Ignition ‘S&#x27;) starting motor terminals.</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/13r00211.png\" alt=\"13r00211.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Arrow Indicating the Flat Side of the Starting motor Mounting Flange.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/13r00212.png\" alt=\"13r00212.png\"></figure><p>Figure 2, Magnetic Switch Orientations. Left: QSK. Right: KV.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Magnetic Switch Bracket Part Numbers</th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Engine</td><td>Engine bank</td><td>Dual</td><td>Single</td></tr><tr><td>KV</td><td>Left</td><td>6377449</td><td>6377447</td></tr><tr><td>Right</td><td>6377448</td><td>6377446</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>QSK</td><td>Left</td><td>6377445</td><td>6377435</td></tr><tr><td>Right</td><td>6377445</td><td>6377435</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/13r00213.png\" alt=\"13r00213.png\"></figure><p>Figure 3, Magnetic Switch Exploded Diagram.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Torque</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Number</td><td>Joint Description</td><td>Torque (Nm)</td></tr><tr><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3913638\">3913638</a></td><td>Mounting Bracket</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>M5 starting motor Ignition (S/T50C) Terminal</td><td>2 to 2.5</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/13r00214.png\" alt=\"13r00214.png\"></figure><p>Figure 4, Starting Motor Terminal Identification. Left: QSK. Right: KV.</p><ol><li>T50C (Ignition ‘S&#x27;) Terminal – <b>Must</b> be balanced using 16AWG wire.</li><li>T30 (B+) Terminal – Outboard most terminal.</li><li>T31 (GND) Terminal – Longer of the two Terminals.</li></ol><p><b>Service Parts Availability</b></p><p>Service parts are available. See Table 3 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 3, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>KV / QSK</td><td>Manufacturer Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>MOTOR, STARTING</td><td>QSK</td><td>(MS7)</td><td>3632273</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>5541784</td></tr><tr><td>3632274</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>5541783</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>KV</td><td>(MS7)</td><td>3632273</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>5542145</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>3632274</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>5538989</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>QSK</td><td>(M125)</td><td>3647165</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>5541784</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>3647166</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>5541783</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>KV</td><td>(M125)</td><td>3647165</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>5542145</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>3647166</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>5538989</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>(M128)</td><td>5541783</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>5541784</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>5538989</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>5542145</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>BRACKET, MAGNETIC SWITCH</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>6377449</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>6377448</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>6377445</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>6377445</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>6377447</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>6377446</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>6377435</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>6377435</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>SCREW, CAP</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3913638\">3913638</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Compatibility</b></p><ul><li>Full compatibility when used in single starting motor applications.</li><li>No compatibility when used in dual or triple starting motor applications.</li><li>The obsolete MS7 and M125 are hard engagement starting motor. The M128 starting motor is a soft engagement starting motor. Use of hard and soft engagement starting motors together is <b>not</b> allowed.</li></ul><p><b>Part Inventory Action</b></p><p>Use existing part number inventory before using the new part number.</p><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 4.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 4, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>33228264</td><td>29 April 2022</td><td>Daventry</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on the engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb220211":"<h2>New Two Stage Turbocharger Frame</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QSK60 CM2150 MCRS</li><li>QSK60 CM850 MCRS</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><p>A new two stage turbocharger frame has been introduced with improved weld geometry. This change to the welds allows an enhanced distribution of the stresses throughout the component reducing high stress areas and cracks.</p><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><p>Two stage turbocharger frame cracks have been reported. A factory investigation identified the initiation points of these cracks to be either at the weld or a high stress location adjacent to the joint.</p><p><b>Service Parts Availability</b></p><p>Service parts are available. See Table 1 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Two Stage frame</td><td>3642933</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6311456</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Inventory Action</b></p><p>Use existing part number inventory before using the new part number.</p><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 2.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN Last</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>33229584</td><td>31 August 2022</td><td>Daventry</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on the engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb220212":"<h2>Industrial Low Temperature Aftercooler (LTA) Water Pump Repair Kits</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QST30 CM552</li><li>QST30 CM2350 T101</li><li>QST30 CM2350 T103C</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><p>This document announces new LTA water pump repair kit part numbers.</p><p><b>Service Parts Availability</b></p><p>Service parts are available. See Table 1 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Repair Kits for LTA Water Pump, Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5409320\">5409320</a></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Number</td><td>Part Name</td></tr><tr><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/6389213\">6389213</a></td><td>KIT,WATER PUMP REPAIR (MINOR)</td></tr><tr><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/6388354\">6388354</a></td><td>KIT,WATER PUMP REPAIR (MAJOR)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Identification</b></p><ul><li>Table 2 documents a complete parts list for both repair kits and Figure 1 shows part location.</li><li>Some parts within the LTA water pump, Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5409320\">5409320</a>, are available for individual purchase. Reference Table 3 for a complete list.</li></ul><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Parts List for Repair Kit, Part Numbers <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/6389213\">6389213</a> and <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/6388354\">6388354</a></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Item Number in Figure 1</td><td>Name</td><td>Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/6389213\">6389213</a></td><td>Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/6388354\">6388354</a></td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>LIPSEAL</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>DEEP GROVE BALL BEARING</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>INTERNAL BEVELLED CIRCLIP</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>O RING</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>PUMP SHAFT</td><td>-</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>BALL BEARING</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>SHIM WASHER</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>LIPSEAL</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>O RING</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>O RING</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>SHAFT SLEEVE</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>MECHANICAL SEAL</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>CHEVRON IMPELLER</td><td>-</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>ORING</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>V CLAMP</td><td>2</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>GASKET</td><td>2</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 3, Purchasable Parts Within LTA Water Pump</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>SCREW,SOCKET HEAD CAP</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4025372\">4025372</a></td></tr><tr><td>WASHER, SPRING</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4025373\">4025373</a></td></tr><tr><td>SEAL, O RING</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4025374\">4025374</a></td></tr><tr><td>SEAL, O RING</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4025376\">4025376</a></td></tr><tr><td>CLAMP, V BAND</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4025377\">4025377</a></td></tr><tr><td>SEAL, WATER PUMP</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4025378\">4025378</a></td></tr><tr><td>SCREW,SOCKET HEAD CAP</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5416040\">5416040</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/08r00578.png\" alt=\"08r00578.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, LTA Water Pump.</p><p><b>Associated Publications</b></p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 4, Associated Publications</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Manual Type</td><td>Engine</td><td>Bulletin Number</td><td>Procedure Title</td><td>Procedure</td><td>Section</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QST30 CM552</td><td><a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/4021539\">4021539</a></td><td>Low-Temperature (LTA) Water Pump</td><td>Refer to Procedure 008-109</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QST30 CM2350 T101</td><td>5467179</td><td>Low-Temperature (LTA) Water Pump</td><td>Refer to Procedure 008-109</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QST30 CM2350 T103C</td><td>6309062</td><td>Low-Temperature (LTA) Water Pump</td><td>Refer to Procedure 008-109</td><td>8</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb220218":"<h2>New Lubricating Oil Pan Suction Tube and Assembly</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QSK60 CM2150 MCRS</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><ul><li>The manufacturing of the lubricating oil pan suction tube has been improved to achieve better control of the critical dimensions.</li><li>All capscrews connecting the supporting brackets to the cylinder block <b>must</b> now have a washer installed to reduce the stresses in the supporting bracket. If this step is <b>not</b> completed, a crack can develop in the joint reducing the pre-load available to support the tube.</li><li>A new material has been introduced on the U Clamps to prevent yielding of the material during installation. The new design creates a greater clamping load on the tube, removing some of the system excitation.</li></ul><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><ul><li>Mid-life inspections have identified cracked lubricating oil suction tubes and large vertical brackets. A factory led project identified the tube manufacturing required improvements to its geometry as the existing process caused the component to remain in high stress once installed.</li><li>The project also identified the brackets would yield when the cylinder block mating capscrew is torqued reducing the pre-load on the joint. To prevent these occurring, washers have been added to all these connections. Yielding was also found on the small horizontal brackets which increased movement of the suction tube; this has been resolved by changing to a stronger U clamp material.</li></ul><p><b>Service Instructions</b></p><ul><li>At engine rebuild the lubricating oil suction tube is to be inspected for the “A” stamping.</li><li>Cummins Inc. recommends lubricating oil suction tubes that do <b>not</b> have the “A” stamping are <b>not</b> to be re-used and should be replaced with a new lubricating oil suction tube marked with the “A” stamping.</li></ul><p><b>Service Parts Availability</b></p><p>Service parts are available. See Table 1 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Washer</td><td>-</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3089555\">3089555</a></td></tr><tr><td>U-Clamp</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3171711\">3171711</a></td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6315230</td></tr><tr><td>Bolts</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3903990\">3903990</a></td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3902460\">3902460</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul><li>The assembly already contains capscrew, Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3902460\">3902460</a>, however the quantity of capscrews required has now increased from eight to twelve bolts.</li><li>Lubricating oil suction tube part number has <b>not</b> changed for the lubricating oil suction tube. The improved lubricating oil suction tube can be identified by an “A” stamping at the end of the part number.</li></ul><p><b>Part Identification</b></p><p>The improved Lubricating oil pan suction tube can be identified by the “A” stamping on the component. The remaining parts can be identified by their individual part number stamping.</p><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 2.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>33229817</td><td>26 August 2022</td><td>Daventry</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on the engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb220223":"<h2>New Heatshield Capscrew</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QSK60 CM2150 MCRS</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><p>This document announces a new capscrew for the heatshield. New capscrew is longer to achieve a more robust joint.</p><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><p>Heatshield caprscrews have been found to be missing or loose, to achieve an improved bolted joint, a new capscrew/torque combination will be implemented.</p><p><b>Service Instructions</b></p><p>The new capscrew torqued up to 54 N•m [ 40 ft-lb ].</p><p><b>Service Parts Availability</b></p><p>Service parts are available. See Table 1 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Capscrew</td><td>3643856</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>5538544</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Identification</b></p><ul><li>Part number 5538544 require a 10mm socket.</li><li>Part number 3643856 requires a 16mm socket.</li></ul><p><b>Publications Affected</b></p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 3, Publications Affected</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Manual Type</td><td>Engine</td><td>Bulletin Number</td><td>Procedure Title</td><td>Procedure</td><td>Section</td></tr><tr><td>Operations and maintenance manual</td><td>QSK60 CM2150 MCRS</td><td><a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/3666260\">3666260</a></td><td>Heat Shield</td><td><a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/56-011-032\">Refer to Procedure 011-032</a></td><td>4</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb220231":"<h2>Updated Turbocharger Service Parts for Turbine to Bearing Housing Bolted Joint</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QSK60 CM500</li><li>QSK60 CM850 MCRS</li><li>QSK60 CM2150 MCRS</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K116</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K121</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K135</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K136</li></ul><p><b>Issue Summary</b></p><p>Symptom:</p><ul><li>Broken, loose, or missing turbine to bearing housing capscrews <b>only</b> on HE800FG turbochargers</li></ul><p><b>Verification</b></p><p>Verify unit is equipped with HE800FG turbochargers utilizing either 16 mm [ 0.630 in ] or 25 mm [ 0.984 in ] length turbine to bearing housing capscrews by measuring the length of the capscrew. If the capscrew length is found to be 16 mm [ 0.630 in ], verify if the turbocharger is equipped with a CSeal. See Figure 1 for identification of a turbocharger with a CSeal versus a turbocharger that does <b>not</b> contain a CSeal.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/09800007.png\" alt=\"09800007.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, CSeal Turbocharger Identification</p><p><b>Resolution</b></p><p>Lockplates installed under B16 or Inconel capscrews improve torque retention of HE800FG turbochargers and reduce susceptibility to malfunction.</p><p><b>Service Instructions</b></p><ul><li>In the event of a HE800FG Turbocharger having broken, loose, or missing turbine to bearing housing capscrews, all eight (8) turbine to bearing capscrews <b>must</b> be replaced.</li><li>Capscrew replacements will require the installation of lockplates underneath the capscrews. When the lockplate tabs are bent upwards after torquing the bolts, the lockplate tabs <b>must</b> seat on the flat sides of the capscrew head and <b>mustnot</b> be seated on the corner of the capscrew head. Refer to Figure 2. Additional torquing past the specified value up to a maximum of 60 degrees is allowed to align the next capscrew head flat with the lockplate tabs.</li><li>If the turbocharger was originally fitted with 25 mm [ 0.984 in ] capscrews, 25 mm [ 0.984 in ] length capscrews <b>must</b> be used for replacement. The 25mm capscrew material will depend on the Service Model Name and will be either B16 or Inconel. Any turbocharger configuration utilizing 25 mm [0.984 in] capscrews <b>must not</b> be fitted with a shim between the flange of the bearing housing and turbine housing. Reference Table 1 for the appropriate bolted joint type for the engine based on the Service Model Name.</li><li>If the turbocharger was originally fitted with 16 mm [ 0.630 in ] capscrews, 16 mm [ 0.630 in ] B16 capscrews with lockplates underneath <b>must</b> be used for replacement, available for order as a service kit. On turbochargers that do <b>not</b> use the CSeal, a 0.25 mm [ 0.010 in ] shim <b>must</b> be fitted between the flange of the bearing housing and the turbine housing. See Figure 3 (ordered separately). Turbochargers that use the CSeal <b>mustnot</b> use a shim between the flange of the bearing housing and turbine housing.</li><li>25 mm [ 0.984 in ] capscrews of either B16 or Inconel material and 16 mm [ 0.630 in ] capscrews installed on CSeal turbochargers requires double torquing of the bolts which is critical to verifying the proper torque retention of the bolted joint. 16 mm [ 0.630 in ] capscrews installed on turbochargers that do <b>not</b> use a CSeal do <b>not</b> require double torquing of the bolts. Torque the capscrews in the sequence shown in Figure 4 with the torque values shown in Table 1.</li><li>Turbocharger capscrew replacements can be completed in-chassis if service access is available to complete the torquing sequence appropriately. If access is inhibited, remove turbocharger to make this repair.</li><li>If 25 mm [ 0.984 in ] Inconel capscrews are unavailable, the recommended countermeasure is to install 25 mm [ 0.984 in ] B16 capscrews with lockplates. The same length capscrew <b>must</b> be used for replacement. See Table 1.</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/08j00467.png\" alt=\"08j00467.png\"></figure><p>Figure 2, Lockplate Bent Tab Requirements: Left: Tab Bent Over Capscrew Head Corner (Unacceptable) Right: Tab Bent Over Capscrew Head Flat (Acceptable).</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/10j00005.png\" alt=\"10j00005.png\"></figure><p>Figure 3, Shim Location</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/10j00223.png\" alt=\"10j00223.png\"></figure><p>Figure 4, Capscrews Torque Sequence.</p><p>Tighten capscrews as shown in Figure 4.</p><p><b>Service Parts</b></p><p>Service parts are offered. See Table 1 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts and Torque Values</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applicable Service Model Names</td><td>Bolted Joint Type</td><td>Torque Value</td><td>Service Part(s)</td><td>Service Part Description</td></tr><tr><td>QSK60 CM500 QSK60 CM850 MCRS QSK60 CM2150 MCRS</td><td>16 mm [ 0.630 in ] B16 with Lockplate</td><td>1. 20.3 N•m [ 15.0 ft-lb ] 2. 20.3 N•m [ 15.0 ft-lb ] + Up to 60° additional</td><td>5357484</td><td>Kit, Service (contains 8 Capscrews and 4 Lockplates)</td></tr><tr><td>25 mm [ 0.984 in ] B16 with Lockplate</td><td>1. 26 N•m [ 19.2 ft-lb ] 2. 26 N•m [ 19.2 ft-lb ] + Up to 60° additional</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5460104\">5460104</a></td><td>25 mm [ 0.984 in ] B16 Capscrew (Individually Sold - quantity 8 needed)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3522904\">3522904</a></td><td>Lockplate (Individually Sold - quantity 4 needed)</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>16 mm [ 0.630 in ] B16 with Lockplate and Shim (Non-CSeal Turbocharger Only)</td><td>1. 20.3 N•m [ 15.0 ft-lb ] + Up to 60° additional</td><td>5357484</td><td>Kit, Service (contains 8 Capscrews and 4 Lockplates)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>3780450</td><td>Shim (quantity 1 needed)</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>QSK60 CM2350 K116 QSK60 CM2350 K121 QSK60 CM2350 K135 QSK60 CM2350 K136</td><td>25 mm [ 0.984 in ] Inconel</td><td>1. 40 N•m [ 30 ft-lb ] 2. 40 N•m [ 30 ft-lb ] + Up to 60° additional</td><td>6417369</td><td>25 mm [ 0.984 in ] Inconel Capscrew Service Kit (Contains 8 Capscrews)</td></tr><tr><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3522904\">3522904</a></td><td>Lockplate (Individually Sold - quantity 4 needed)</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/09y00003.png\" alt=\"09y00003.png\"></figure><p>Figure 5, Visual Example of Bolted Joint Architecture for 16 mm [ 0.630 in ] B16 with Lockplate and 25 mm [ 0.984 in ] B16 with Lockplate turbochargers for QSK60 CM500, QSK60 CM850 MCRS, and QSK60 CM2150 MCRS Service Model Names</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/10j00003.png\" alt=\"10j00003.png\"></figure><p>Figure 6, Visual Example of Bolted Joint Architecture for 16 mm [ 0.630 in ] B16 with Lockplate and Shim (Non-CSeal Turbo Only) turbochargers for QSK60 CM500, QSK60 CM850 MCRS, and QSK60 CM2150 MCRS Service Model Names</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/09y00003.png\" alt=\"09y00003.png\"></figure><p>Figure 7, Visual Example of Bolted Joint Architecture for 25 mm [ 0.984 in ] Inconel with Lockplate turbochargers for QSK60 CM2350 K116, QSK60 CM2350 K121, QSK60 CM2350 K135, and QSK60 CM2350 K136 Service Model Name</p><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb220234":"<h2>New Eliminator Casting Body</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QSK60 CM2150 MCRS</li><li>QSK60 CM2250</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K116</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K117</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K121</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K135</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K136</li><li>QSK60 CM500</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><p>This document announces a new eliminator body design. New design has increased flange thickness and subsequently bolt length increases.</p><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><p>An external oil leak has occurred on multiple machines, stemming from cracks around the bolt heads on the eliminator body (this is where the eliminator body bolts to the engine cylinder block). To improve reliability, a new oil eliminator body has been released to reduce the stresses at the crack location.</p><p><b>Service Instructions</b></p><p>New capscrew, Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3902663\">3902663</a>, <b>must</b> be used when fitting the new eliminator, Part Number 6373355. See Table 1 for part number information.</p><p><b>Service Parts Availability</b></p><p>Service parts are available. See Table 1 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Eliminator filter</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5540796\">5540796</a></td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6373355</td></tr><tr><td>Screw, hexagon flange head cap</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3914407\">3914407</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3902663\">3902663</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Compatibility</b></p><p>New capscrew, Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3902663\">3902663</a>, <b>must</b> be used when fitting the new eliminator, Part Number 6373355. See Table 1 for part number information.</p><p><b>Part Identification</b></p><p>The eliminator part number can be identified by the data plate on the component.</p><p><b>Part Inventory Action</b></p><p>Use existing part number inventory before using the new part number.</p><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 2.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>33230192</td><td>26 October 2022</td><td>Daventry</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on the engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb220253":"<h2>New Insulated Flexplate</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QSK60 CM2150 MCRS</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><p>A new flexplate is now available and includes the following:</p><ul><li>Clamping plate rather than individual bushings at the crankshaft adapter to resin interface; this reduces the torsional vibration and bending loads absorbed by this joint increasing reliability.</li><li>Press fit on the internal diameter allowing the alternator weight to be absorbed by this joint, reducing stresses in the clamping plate.</li><li>Capscrew quantity has been increased from 15 to 20; this increases the torsional load capability.</li><li>Increased crankshaft adapter length to provide increased support to the bolted joint area.</li></ul><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><p>Increased vibration and adverse noises during operation have been reported. The insulated flexplate membrane can move independently to the crankshaft adapter. An investigation identified this bolted joint experiences high torsional loading which caused fretting and enlarging of the holes.</p><p><b>Customer Communication</b></p><p>This flexplate is <b>only</b> required on Hitachi haul truck application.</p><p><b>Service Parts Availability</b></p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Insulated Flexplate</td><td>5541000</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6315834</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Identification</b></p><p>Part can be identified by the sticker located on the membrane. See Figure 1.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/16r00149.png\" alt=\"16r00149.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Flexplate Identification.</p><p><b>Part Inventory Action</b></p><p>Use existing part number inventory before using the new part number.</p><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 2.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>33230674</td><td>16 December 2022</td><td>Daventry</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on the engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb230007":"<h2>New Fuel Pump Accessory Drive and Electronic Chip Detector</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QSK60 CM2150</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K116</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K117</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K121</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K135</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K136</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><ul><li>A new fuel pump accessory drive has been released.</li><li>The new fuel pump accessory drive introduces an electronic chip detector in the fuel pump oil drain path to identify any metallic debris being created by a failing fuel pump.</li><li>In the event metallic debris is detected by the electronic chip detector a signal will be sent to the engine control module (ECM) to take appropriate action to protect the engine and fuel pump.</li></ul><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><ul><li>Malfunctions of modular common rail system fuel pumps have been reported. The root cause of malfunction has been identified as debris in the fuel pump.</li><li>The electronic chip detector system will detect metallic debris and flakes being generated by the fuel pump. The electronic chip detector system was designed as an early warning of fuel pump failure, minimizing the chance of other engine components becoming affected by the metallic flakes entering the engine lubricating oil system.</li></ul><p><b>Service Instructions</b></p><p>To effectively utilize this new fuel pump accessory drive, the electronic chip detector <b>must</b> be connected to the engine wiring harness. Follow the installation instructions below.</p><p>Installation Instructions:</p><ul><li>Connect extension harness, Part number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5542339\">5542339</a>, to left bank main engine wiring harness as shown in Figure 1.</li><li>Install two P clips as shown in Figure 1.</li></ul><div class=\"callout tip\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">🔧</span>Момент затяжки · Torque Value</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>33 n•m [24 ft-lb]</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/05r00318.png\" alt=\"05r00318.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Wiring Harness Connection and P-clip Locations.</p><ul><li>Install two P-clips to aftercooler in an inboard orientation as shown in Figure 2.</li></ul><div class=\"callout tip\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">🔧</span>Момент затяжки · Torque Value</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>45 n•m [33 ft-lb]</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/05r00319.png\" alt=\"05r00319.png\"></figure><p>Figure 2, P-clip Locations on Aftercooler.</p><ul><li>Install one P-clip to bottom aftercooler bolt as shown in Figure 3.</li></ul><div class=\"callout tip\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">🔧</span>Момент затяжки · Torque Value</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>45 n•m [33 ft-lb]</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/05r00320.png\" alt=\"05r00320.png\"></figure><p>Figure 3, P-clip Location on Aftercooler.</p><ul><li>Install two P-clips to the fuel pump accessory drive as shown in Figure 4.</li></ul><div class=\"callout tip\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">🔧</span>Момент затяжки · Torque Value</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>45 n•m [33 ft-lb]</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/05r00321.png\" alt=\"05r00321.png\"></figure><p>Figure 4, P-clip Location on Fuel Pump Accessory Drive.</p><ul><li>Connect wiring harness to electronic chip detector.</li></ul><p><b>Service Parts Availability</b></p><p>Service parts are available. See Table 1 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Accessory drive</td><td>5417132</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5541365\">5541365</a></td></tr><tr><td>Electronic Chip Detector</td><td>-</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/6372680\">6372680</a></td></tr><tr><td>Extension Harness</td><td>-</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5542339\">5542339</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Compatibility</b></p><ul><li>If the engine is equipped with existing thermistor style exhaust gas temperature sensors, the existing thermistor style exhaust gas temperature sensors <b>must</b> be replaced with thermocouple style exhaust gas temperature sensors. Reference <a class=\"lnk tsb\" href=\"#/doc/tsb210002\">TSB210002</a> for details.</li><li>To utilize this electronic chip detector a change of main engine wiring harnesses is required to part numbers mentioned in <a class=\"lnk tsb\" href=\"#/doc/tsb210002\">TSB210002</a>. This is required as prior part numbers do <b>not</b> contain the connection required for the electronic chip detector.</li></ul><p><b>Part Identification</b></p><p>Parts can be identified by part number stamped on the fuel pump accessory drive and electronic chip detector. The extension wiring harness contains a tag with the part number.</p><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 2.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>33230337</td><td>14 November 2022</td><td>Daventry</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb230019":"<h2>New ReCon® Aftertreatment NOx Sensors Causing Fault Code 3232 or 2771</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>All products utilizing an aftertreatment NOx sensor</li></ul><p><b>Issue Summary</b></p><p>Symptom:</p><ul><li>After installation of a “new” ReCon® (RX) aftertreatment intake NOx sensor, Fault Code 3232 is active.</li><li>After installation of a “new” ReCon® (RX) aftertreatment outlet NOx sensor, Fault Code 2771 is active.</li></ul><p>Root Cause:</p><ul><li>An aftertreatment NOx sensor quality issue was identified in a limited number of sensors manufactured before 19 January 2023.</li></ul><p><b>Verification</b></p><p>Fault Code 3232 or 2771 is active at key-on immediately after installing a “new” ReCon® (RX) aftertreatment NOx sensor. This issue <b>only</b> affects ReCon® (RX) aftertreatment NOx sensors (Part numbers are listed in Table 1) that have gone through the remanufacturing process. These aftertreatment NOx sensors can be identified by the inline connector that is present in the aftertreatment NOx sensor probe wiring as shown in Figure 1.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Affected ReCon® Aftertreatment NOx Sensor Part Numbers</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Number</td><td>Sensor Description</td></tr><tr><td>4326862RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Intake NOx Sensor</td></tr><tr><td>4326865RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Intake NOx Sensor</td></tr><tr><td>4326867RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Intake NOx Sensor</td></tr><tr><td>4326870RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Intake NOx Sensor</td></tr><tr><td>4326873RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Intake NOx Sensor</td></tr><tr><td>4327153RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Intake NOx Sensor</td></tr><tr><td>3687930RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Intake NOx Sensor</td></tr><tr><td>3688978RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Intake NOx Sensor</td></tr><tr><td>5289662RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Intake NOx Sensor</td></tr><tr><td>5295473RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Intake NOx Sensor</td></tr><tr><td>4326861RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Outlet NOx Sensor</td></tr><tr><td>4326863RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Outlet NOx Sensor</td></tr><tr><td>4326864RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Outlet NOx Sensor</td></tr><tr><td>4326866RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Outlet NOx Sensor</td></tr><tr><td>4326868RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Outlet NOx Sensor</td></tr><tr><td>4326869RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Outlet NOx Sensor</td></tr><tr><td>4326871RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Outlet NOx Sensor</td></tr><tr><td>4326872RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Outlet NOx Sensor</td></tr><tr><td>4326874RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Outlet NOx Sensor</td></tr><tr><td>5419105RX</td><td>ReCon® Aftertreatment Outlet NOx Sensor</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/19r99901.png\" alt=\"19r99901.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Aftertreatment NOx Sensor Probe Inline Connector.</p><p><b>Resolution</b></p><p>Replace the aftertreatment NOx sensor. See corresponding Service Manual. Reference Procedure 019-451 or 019-463 in Section 19.</p><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb230043":"<h2>Injector / Exhaust Gas Temperature Sensor (EGTS) Harness Bracket Conversion and Cable Tie Tension Optimisation to Improve Harness Restraint</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QSK60 MCRS (All Versions)</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><ul><li>The injector / EGTS harness bracket has been updated to include quantity 4 of 6.5 mm +/- 0.25 mm drillings to accept quantity 2 cable ties per bracket.</li><li>The tension of cable ties has been increased.</li></ul><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><ul><li>The addition of cable ties to the bracket more positively locates the injector and EGT wiring at each cylinder location, preventing contact with other components.</li><li>Increased cable tie tension more positively locates the injector and EGT wiring at each cylinder location, preventing contact with other components.</li></ul><p><b>Service Parts</b></p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Parts Supersession</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Obsolete Part Number</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>BRACKET, WIRING RETAINER</td><td>5372422</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5539888\">5539888</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Resolution</b></p><p>Existing bracket variants can be converted to the new specification using the following instructions:</p><ol><li>Remove the injector / exhaust gas temperature sensor (EGTS) harness bracket from the engine.</li><li>Locate and centre punch the injector / exhaust gas temperature sensor (EGTS) harness bracket where holes are to be drilled, marked by the dashed boxes in the diagram in Figure 1.</li><li>Secure the work piece is a suitable vice.</li><li>Through drill each location using a 6.5 mm drill bit and suitable drill.</li><li>Debur both sides of each drilling, using a suitable deburring tool.</li><li>Insert cable tie, Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5286859\">5286859</a>, through the drillings in each location on the injector / exhaust gas temperature sensor (EGTS) harness bracket.</li><li>Install the injector / exhaust gas temperature sensor (EGTS) harness bracket to the engine.</li><li>Loosely secure the cable ties around the injector / EGTS harness. Position the harness such that clearance is achieved with the aftercooler cover. Tighten the cable tie. Do <b>not</b> to over-tighten the cable tie or damage to the harness may result.</li><li>Cut off any surplus from the free end of the cable tie, using suitable flush cutters.</li></ol><p>New injector / exhaust gas temperature sensor (EGTS) harness bracket variants can also have the harness position optimised and the cable tie replaced, and tension increased, as described in steps 8 and 9 above.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/19r99906.png\" alt=\"19r99906.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Drilling Locations Indicated by Dashed Boxes.</p><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb230078":"<h2>Leaking Coolant From Low Temperature Aftercooler (LTA) Core</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QSK60 CM850 MCRS</li><li>QSK60 CM2150 K132M</li><li>QSK60 CM2150 MCRS</li><li>QSK60 CM2150 MCRS G</li></ul><p><b>Issue Summary</b></p><p>Symptom:</p><ul><li>Excessive coolant use; external coolant leak</li></ul><p>Root Cause:</p><ul><li>Capscrews used for the aftercooler cores are loose or broken; aftercooler core is cracked</li></ul><p><b>Verification</b></p><p>If a coolant leak from the aftercoolers is observed, or excessive coolant use has been confirmed, follow instructions in resolution section.</p><p><b>Resolution</b></p><ul><li>If a coolant leak from the aftercoolers is verified, replace aftercooler cores and capscrews with new ones.</li><li>A new capscrew part number should be used which allows for a greater torque value. See Table 1 for part numbers.</li><li>For aftercooler core removal and installation instructions, see corresponding Service Manual. Reference Procedure 010-002 in section 10. See Table 3 for Service Manual information.</li></ul><p><b>Service Parts</b></p><p>Service parts are offered. See Table 1 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Aftercooler core</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5543593\">5543593</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td></tr><tr><td>Cap Screw</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3902460\">3902460</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3093932\">3093932</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 2.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>33231598</td><td>Apr 2023</td><td>Daventry Engine Plant</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Publications Affected</b></p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 3, Publications Affected</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Manual Type</td><td>Engine</td><td>Bulletin Number</td><td>Procedure Title</td><td>Procedure</td><td>Section</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QSK60 CM2150 MCRS</td><td><a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/4021530\">4021530</a></td><td>Aftercooler Assembly</td><td>Refer to Procedure 010-002</td><td>10</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb230092":"<h2>Fan Clutch Oil Supply Manifold Solenoid Re-orientation</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QSK50 CM2150 MCRS</li><li>QSK50 CM850 MCRS</li><li>QSK50 CM2350 K108</li><li>QSK50 CM2350 K130</li></ul><p><b>Issue</b></p><p>Fan clutch oil solenoid coil malfunction causes permanent cooling fan engagement. Fault codes 245 and 2377 become active. Permanent full fan speed can cause over-cooling and excessive noise.</p><p><b>Verification</b></p><ul><li>Engine cooling fan runs constantly</li><li>Fault codes 245 and 2377 become active</li></ul><p><b>Resolution</b></p><p>A new fan clutch oil manifold has been designed to improve oil cooling on the oil control solenoid coil. This fan clutch oil manifold is released in a parts kit (part number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/6468466\">6468466</a>). The parts kit contains all the required components to upfit the new manifold and separately available solenoid (part number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3641305\">3641305</a>) to the engine.</p><p>Existing design oil manifold:</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/16r00150.png\" alt=\"16r00150.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Existing Oil Manifold Installation.</p><p>New design oil manifold:</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/16r00151.png\" alt=\"16r00151.png\"></figure><p>Figure 2, New Design Oil Manifold Installation.</p><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><p>The new design oil manifold rotates the solenoid mounting location by 180°. In this orientation, cooling oil is gravity fed to the solenoid coil, reducing coil temperature and therefore the risk of malfunction.</p><p><b>Service Instructions</b></p><p>Order upfit kit, Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/6468466\">6468466</a> and solenoid valve, Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3641305\">3641305</a>, before attempting to carry out this repair. Check part quantities delivered in the parts kit, as per Table 1, Service Parts. Remove the five male – male unions/ adapters from the existing manifold. Re-use these unions/ connectors. O-rings will be provided in the parts kit.</p><p>To replace the fan clutch oil control manifold and solenoid, see corresponding Service Manual. Reference Procedure 007-125 in Section 7.</p><ul><li>GTA38, K38, K50 QSK38 and QSK50 Service Manual, Bulletin <a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/4021528\">4021528</a></li><li>QSK50 CM2350 K108 Service Manual, Bulletin 4332823</li><li>QSK50 CM2350 K130 Service Manual, Bulletin 5504180</li></ul><p>Following installation of the fan clutch oil control manifold and solenoid, the solenoid wiring harness <b>must</b> be re-routed to allow connector alignment with the new solenoid position. This process differs for Tier 2 and Tier 4 engines. Verify the route required and re-route as shown in Figure 5.</p><p>QSK50 CM2150 MCRS Engines:</p><ul><li>Remove the solenoid harness from the shared P-clip, holding the solenoid harness and the engine harness, to the rocker cover (circled yellow). Cut the cable tie securing the solenoid harness to the existing engine harness. Figure 3 shows the existing route for Tier 2 engines.</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/16r00152.png\" alt=\"16r00152.png\"></figure><p>Figure 3, Tier 2 Engine Wiring Harness Routing.</p><p>QSK50 CM2350 K108/ K130 Engines:</p><ul><li>Figure 4 shows the exisiting Tier 4 solenoid harness route, which has an engine harness P-clipped to it on the rocker cover. Remove the solenoid harness from the engine harness P-clip.</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/16r00153.png\" alt=\"16r00153.png\"></figure><p>Figure 4, Tier 4 Engine Wiring Harness Route.</p><p>All Engines:</p><ul><li>Secure existing Tier 2 and Tier 4 non-solenoid harnesses as per the original installation.</li><li>Re-route the solenoid harness to the new solenoid position. Figure 6 shows the required new route using two P-clips (part number 68152A) on existing gear cover capscrew locations across both Tier 2 and Tier 4 engines.</li><li>Remove two gear cover capscrews and install the solenoid harness through the P-clips, vertically upwards as shown.</li><li>Install the capscrews.</li></ul><div class=\"callout tip\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">🔧</span>Момент затяжки · Torque Value</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>75 n•m [55 ft-lb]</p></div></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/16r00154.png\" alt=\"16r00154.png\"></figure><p>Figure 5, New Solenoid Harness Routing.</p><ul><li>Connect the wiring harness to the solenoid plug. Verify a positive “click” as the connector fork slots over the plug and locks in position to avoid backing out.</li></ul><p><b>Service Parts Availability</b></p><p>Service parts are available in kit form. See Table 1 for part numbers contained within the parts kit.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td><td>Part Quantity</td></tr><tr><td>MANIFOLD, OIL</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3640889\">3640889</a></td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/6414133\">6414133</a></td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>SCREW, SOCKET HEAD CAP</td><td>S143B</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>WASHER, LOCK</td><td>S604</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>GASKET, CORROSION RESISTOR</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3650106\">3650106</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>PLUG, PIPE</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3008466\">3008466</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>PLUG, THREADED</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3046202\">3046202</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>PLUG, THREADED</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3037536\">3037536</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>CLIP</td><td>68152A</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>SEAL, O RING</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3348871\">3348871</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>SEAL, O RING</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3627695\">3627695</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>SEAL, O RING</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3089240\">3089240</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>SEAL, O RING</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3630740\">3630740</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>SEAL, O RING</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3922794\">3922794</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>SEAL, O RING</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3037537\">3037537</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>-</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Compatibility</b></p><p>The new oil manifold is common across the QSK50 Tier 2 and Tier 4, as was the existing design. New and existing oil manifolds are backwards compatible. The new manifold is advised for expected fan solenoid operation and service life.</p><p><b>Part Identification</b></p><p>The new oil manifold is different in shape to the existing part, allowing the oil control solenoid to sit upside down. The new part number is stamped in the location shown in Figure 6 The existing part number is stamped exactly 180° opposite to this on the bottom face. Both part numbers are stamped on the face opposite to the solenoid when viewed as installed.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/16r00155.png\" alt=\"16r00155.png\"></figure><p>Figure 6, Part Number Stamping Location on New Fan Clutch Oil Manifold.</p><p><b>Part Inventory Action</b></p><p>Obsolete part numbers in stock are <b>not</b> to be used.</p><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 2.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>33231741</td><td>8 April 2023</td><td>Daventry Engine Plant</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Publications Affected</b></p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 3, Publications Affected</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Manual Type</td><td>Engine</td><td>Bulletin Number</td><td>Procedure Title</td><td>Procedure</td><td>Section</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QSK50 CM2150 MCRS</td><td><a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/4021528\">4021528</a></td><td>Fan Clutch Lubricating Oil Manifold</td><td><a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-007-125\">Refer to Procedure 007-125</a></td><td>7</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QSK50 CM2350 K130</td><td>5504180</td><td>Fan Clutch Lubricating Oil Manifold</td><td>Refer to Procedure 007-125</td><td>7</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QSK50 CM2350 K108</td><td>4332823</td><td>Fan Clutch Lubricating Oil Manifold</td><td>Refer to Procedure 007-125</td><td>7</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb230094":"<h2>New Engine Paint</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>K38 (All Versions)</li><li>K50 (All Versions)</li><li>QSK38 (All Versions)</li><li>QSK45 (All Versions)</li><li>QSK50 (All Versions)</li><li>QSK60 (All Versions)</li><li>QSK78 (All Versions)</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><p>Switching from oil-based paint to a new water-based paint which is now being used on all Daventry Engine Plant manufactured engines. The change was effective on the 03 January 2023.</p><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><p>Paint defects such as peeling, cracking, and fading during application have been observed. The new water-based paint has better thermal capabilities, has performed better over extended use in applications, and is more environmentally friendly.</p><p><b>Customer Communication</b></p><p>No new aftermarket touch-up water-based paint is available. Existing aftermarket paint is oil-based and there may be visual differences in shade and finish. Reference Table 1 for the applicable engineering standards (use Cummins® Engineering Standards and RAL codes to request water-based touch-up paint from an external supplier).</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Paint Description and Color Standard</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Color Description</td><td>Color standard</td></tr><tr><td>CUMMINS BEIGE, HIGH GLOSS</td><td>CES 60027</td></tr><tr><td>CUMMINS RED, HIGH GLOSS</td><td>CES 60027</td></tr><tr><td>CUMMINS MARINE GRAY, HIGH GLOSS</td><td>CES 60027</td></tr><tr><td>CUMMINS BLACK, HIGH GLOSS</td><td>CES 60027</td></tr><tr><td>CUMMINS ONAN GREEN, HIGH GLOSS</td><td>CES 60027</td></tr><tr><td>CUMMINS CLOUD WHITE, HIGH GLOSS</td><td>CES 60027</td></tr><tr><td>KOMATSU YELLOW</td><td>*</td></tr><tr><td>TRAFFIC GRAY</td><td>RAL7043</td></tr><tr><td>BASALT GRAY</td><td>RAL7012</td></tr><tr><td>*Komatsu engineering standard</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Because of the difference in viscosity between water-based and aerosol oil-based paints, casting imperfections will be more noticeable.</p><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 2.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Paint color</td><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>Beige</td><td>33230671</td><td>1 March 2023</td><td>Daventry</td></tr><tr><td>Red</td><td>33230409</td><td>1 April 2023</td><td>Daventry</td></tr><tr><td>Green</td><td>33230678</td><td>1 March 2023</td><td>Daventry</td></tr><tr><td>Marine Grey</td><td>33229985</td><td>1 March 2023</td><td>Daventry</td></tr><tr><td>Komatsu Yellow</td><td>33230291</td><td>1 March 2023</td><td>Daventry</td></tr><tr><td>Black</td><td>33230443</td><td>1 April 2023</td><td>Daventry</td></tr><tr><td>Basalt (Terex) Grey</td><td>33230958</td><td>2 March 2023</td><td>Daventry</td></tr><tr><td>Liebherr (Traffic) Grey</td><td>33230695</td><td>1 May 2023</td><td>Daventry</td></tr><tr><td>Cloud White</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>Daventry</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on the engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb230096":"<h2>ECM Reset and Communication Issue Troubleshooting</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QST30 CM552</li></ul><p><b>Issue Summary</b></p><p>Symptom:</p><ul><li>ECM <b>not</b> able to communicate upon engine key on</li></ul><p>Root Cause:</p><ul><li>ECM issue</li></ul><p><b>Verification</b></p><p>Use the following steps to diagnose ECM:</p><ol><li>With Calterm® (preferred) or INSITE™ electronic service tool and a datalink adapter hooked up to a suspect ECM.</li><li>Set battery voltage at 26V to 28V.</li><li>Key on and verify if the ECM will communicate. Key off. Repeat a few times.</li><li>If the ECM will <b>not</b> communicate key off and reduce battery voltage to 12V to 14V.</li><li>Key on and see if the ECM will communicate.</li><li>If the ECM will <b>not</b> communicate at the higher voltage but does at the lower voltage, the ECM likely has the reset issue.</li><li>A malfunctioning ECM has been detected when keyed on at a higher voltage and <b>not</b> communicating will be continuously resetting.</li></ol><div class=\"callout note\"><div class=\"callout-head\"><span class=\"callout-ico\">i</span>Note · Примечание</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>If using Calterm® and can communicate, check the reset parameter NRESETSE and see how many resets have occurred. A normally functioning module should <b>not</b> have any or <b>only</b> a few resets. Improper shutdowns (removing battery voltage in less than 30 sec after keying off) will increment the reset count each occurrence. The longer the ECM is left on and <b>not</b> communicating the higher the reset count will be when the ECM does communicate at the lower voltage. The reset count can increment by hundreds in just a few minutes.</p></div></div><p>Fault codes that can be seen for suspect ECMs are listed below:</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Fault Codes</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fault Code</td><td>Description</td></tr><tr><td>285</td><td>SAE J1939 Multiplexing PGN Timeout Error - Abnormal Update Rate</td></tr><tr><td>346</td><td>Engine Control Module Warning Software error - Bad intelligent device or component</td></tr><tr><td>426</td><td>SAE J1939 Datalink - Data Erratic, Intermittent, or Incorrect; J1939 Network #1 - Data erratic, intermittent or incorrect</td></tr><tr><td>434</td><td>Power Lost With Ignition On - Data Erratic, Intermittent, or Incorrect</td></tr><tr><td>1117</td><td>Power Lost With Ignition On - Data Erratic, Intermittent, or Incorrect</td></tr><tr><td>1135</td><td>SAE J1939 Data Link 2 Engine Network - Abnormal Update Rate</td></tr><tr><td>2727</td><td>SAE J1939 Data Link 2 Engine Network - Abnormal Update Rate</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Resolution</b></p><p>Replace malfunctioning ECM. See QST30 Industrial Electronic Control System Troubleshooting and Repair Manual, Bulletin <a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/3666214\">3666214</a>. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/87-019-031\">Refer to Procedure 019-031 in Section 19.</a></p><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb230138":"<h2>New Coolant Thermostat</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QSK50 CM2150 MCRS</li><li>QSK60 CM2150 K132M</li><li>QSK60 CM2150 MCRS M</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K117</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K121</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K135</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K149M</li></ul><p><b>Issue Summary</b></p><p>Symptom:</p><ul><li>Engine shut down due to Fault Code 146 Engine coolant temperature is above the engine protection warning limit.</li></ul><p>Root Cause:</p><ul><li>Coolant thermostats are damaged or stuck closed.</li></ul><p><b>Verification</b></p><p>Inspect coolant thermostats for damage. If coolant thermostats are found to be damaged or stuck closed, follow instructions in resolution section.</p><p><b>Resolution</b></p><ul><li>If coolant thermostats are found to be damaged or stuck closed, replace with new part number in Table 1.</li><li>For coolant thermostat removal and installation instructions, see corresponding Service Manual. Reference Procedure 008-013 in Section 8.</li></ul><p><b>Service Parts</b></p><p>Service parts are offered. See Table 1 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Coolant Thermostat</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3629205\">3629205</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5538724\">5538724</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb230152":"<h2>New Lubricating Oil pump and Lubricating Oil Pump Regulator Spring</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QSK45 CM500 K158G</li><li>QSK60 CM500 K159G</li><li>QSK60 CM2150 MCRS</li><li>QSK60 CM2150 MCRS G</li><li>QSK60 CM2150 MCRS M</li><li>QSK60 CM2150 K132M</li><li>QSK60 CM2250 K112</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K116</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K117</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K121</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K133</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K135</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K136</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K149M</li><li>QSK78 CM500 K160G</li><li>QSK78 CM2250 K104</li><li>QSK78 CM2350 K126</li><li>QSK78 CM2350 K137</li><li>QSK78 CM2350 K154C</li><li>QSK78 CM2450 K161G</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><ul><li>A new lubricating oil pump has been released with a new regulator valve spring.</li><li>To maintain the correct free length and preload the spring is manufactured with a different process than earlier and increased free length specification.</li></ul><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><ul><li>There have been reports of low oil pressure and erratic main oil rifle pressure.</li><li>The change in speed in haul truck applications causes the regulator spring to move repeatedly and causes early relaxation of the spring. To maintain the correct free length and preload, the regulator spring with different manufacturing processes has been implemented.</li></ul><p><b>Service Parts Availability</b></p><p>Service parts are available. See Table 1 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td><td>Quantity Required</td><td>Reference</td></tr><tr><td>SPRING, PRESSURE REGULATOR</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3635142\">3635142</a></td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/6319697\">6319697</a></td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>PUMP, LUBRICATING OIL</td><td>3644520</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6319711</td><td>1</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>PUMP, LUBRICATING OIL</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3644518\">3644518</a></td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/6319710\">6319710</a></td><td>1</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>PUMP, LUBRICATING OIL</td><td>3644516</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6319705</td><td>1</td><td>4</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Identification</b></p><p>The part number is stamped on the part.</p><p><b>Part Inventory Action</b></p><ul><li>Use existing part number inventory before using the new part number.</li><li>Use the new parts in construction applications <b>only</b> until existing inventory stock has been eliminated.</li></ul><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 2.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td><td>Engine Model</td></tr><tr><td>33232528</td><td>12 June 2023</td><td>Daventry</td><td>QSK60</td></tr><tr><td>33232736</td><td>28 June 2023</td><td>Daventry</td><td>QSK45</td></tr><tr><td>66307001</td><td>12 June 2023</td><td>Daventry</td><td>QSK78</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb230161":"<h2>New Turbocharger Bellows: Exhaust Leak</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QSK60 CM2150 MCRS</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><p>There is a new turbocharger bellows part number. The new turbocharger bellows has a smaller internal sleeve allowing improved clearance to the convolutes and exhaust connector. The design of the V-band mating face has also been changed to enable an improved contact with the V-band clamp.</p><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><ul><li>There have been reports of exhaust gas leakage during engine operation. There is inadequate clearance between the turbocharger bellows inner sleeve and convolutes leading to contact. The resulting fretting takes place can either break parts away from the inner sleeve or cause a rupture of the convolute leading to exhaust gas leakage.</li><li>V-band clamps can also become loose during operation. The assignable cause of turbocharger bellows malfunction was found to be the low contact area between the clamp and turbocharger bellows causing high stresses in the V-band clamp leading to component fracture.</li></ul><p><b>Customer Communication</b></p><p>V Band clamp, Part number 201989, <b>must</b> be used or the V-band and/or turbocharger bellows can malfunction.</p><p><b>Service Instructions</b></p><p>Service parts are available. See Table 1.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Bellows</td><td>4100758</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6315830</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Identification</b></p><p>New part can be identified by the part number stamped on turbocharger bellow flange. See Figure 1.</p><p><b>Part Compatibility</b></p><p>Only V Band clamp, Part number 201989, is compatible with the new part number.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/11r00695.png\" alt=\"11r00695.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Part Number Location on Turbocharger Bellows.</p><p><b>Part Inventory Action</b></p><p>Obsolete part numbers in stock are <b>not</b> to be used. Obsolete part numbers in stock are to be returned for reimbursement.</p><p><b>Production Status</b></p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>33232574</td><td>16 June 2023</td><td>Daventry</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on the engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb230167":"<h2>Missing Rocker Lever Rivets</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>KTAA19 K131G</li><li>KTAA38 K134G</li><li>KTA50 K148G</li><li>QSK19 CM850 MCRS</li><li>QSK19 CM2150 K139M</li><li>QSK19 CM2350 K114</li><li>QSK19 CM2350 K144G</li><li>QSK19 CM2350 K167C</li><li>QSK19 CM2450 K155G</li><li>QSK38 CM850 MCRS</li><li>QSK38 CM2150 K142M</li><li>QSK38 CM2350 K125</li><li>QSK38 CM2350 K138M</li><li>QSK38 CM2350 K145G</li><li>QSK50 CM2150 K140M</li><li>QSK50 CM2150 MCRS</li><li>QSK50 CM2150 MCRS G</li><li>QSK50 CM2350 K146G</li><li>QSK50 CM2350 K163C</li></ul><p><b>Issue Summary</b></p><p>Symptom:</p><ul><li>Missing rivet at rocker lever cross drilling</li></ul><p>Root Cause:</p><ul><li>Improper fit between rocker lever drilling inside diameter and rivet outside diameter</li></ul><p><b>Verification</b></p><p>Verification is visual <b>only</b> and does <b>not</b> have to be proactive. During planned maintenance of the overhead, remove rocker lever cover and inspect rocker levers.</p><p><b>Resolution</b></p><ul><li>If a missing rivet is verified, options for replacement are available. The original rivet can be installed in existing rocker lever or rocker lever assembly can be replaced with a new part number with an improved plug. Plug design changed from a rivet to ball plug style. See Table 1 for part number details.</li><li>Replacement of the rivet does <b>not</b> have to be proactive as missing rivet has no impact on oil pressure. Replacement can occur during a planned engine rebuild.</li></ul><p><b>Service Parts</b></p><ul><li>Service parts are offered. See Table 1 for part numbers.</li><li>Rocker lever assembly, top level, and kit part numbers did <b>not</b> change. Part numbers within the kits and assemblies did change. See Table 1 for details.</li></ul><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Lever, Rocker</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3053476\">3053476</a></td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/6377069\">6377069</a></td></tr><tr><td>Lever, Rocker</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3053477\">3053477</a></td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/6377067\">6377067</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 2.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>33233267</td><td>August 2023</td><td>Daventry Engine Plant</td></tr><tr><td>37291483</td><td>August 2023</td><td>Seymour Engine Plant</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb230183":"<h2>New Rear Crankshaft Seal Part Number</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>K38 (All versions)</li><li>K50 (All versions)</li><li>QSK38 (All versions)</li><li>QSK50 (All versions)</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><ul><li>A new rear crankshaft seal has been released which has a larger outer diameter 214.12 mm [ 8.429 in ] than the existing version 206.38 mm [ 8.125 in ].</li><li>Associated flywheel housings have also had part number changes to accommodate the larger diameter crankshaft seal.</li><li>Service Tooling kit part numbers have been updated to accommodate the new design rear crankshaft seal.</li></ul><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><p>The new design of crankshaft seal and service tooling improves installation errors, lube oil sealing, and component longevity.</p><p><b>Service Parts Availability</b></p><p>Service parts are available. See Table 1 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Housing, Flywheel</td><td>3176637</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>5374479</td></tr><tr><td>Housing, Flywheel</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4917572\">4917572</a></td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5374489\">5374489</a></td></tr><tr><td>Housing, Flywheel</td><td>3414620</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>5374494</td></tr><tr><td>Housing, Flywheel</td><td>4978338</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>5374498</td></tr><tr><td>Housing, Flywheel</td><td>3628045</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>5374483</td></tr><tr><td>Housing, Flywheel</td><td>3176637</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>5374479</td></tr><tr><td>Seal, Crank</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3642365\">3642365</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>4396606</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Compatibility</b></p><ul><li>No backwards compatibility exists between part numbers in Table 1.</li><li>Table 2 shows the part compatibility matrix.</li></ul><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Part Compatibility Matrix</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Flywheel Housing</td><td>New Crankshaft Seal, Part Number 4396606 (214.12 mm [ 8.429 in ] Diameter) Compatibility</td><td>Existing Crankshaft Seal, Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3642365\">3642365</a> (206.38 mm [ 8.125 in ] Diameter) Compatibility</td></tr><tr><td>Flywheel Housing (214.12 mm [ 8.429 in ] Bore)</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>Flywheel Housing (206.38 mm [ 8.125 in ] Bore)</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Inventory Action</b></p><ul><li>Use existing part number inventory before using the new part number.</li><li>The existing crankshaft seal will remain available as the existing crankshaft seal is used on other engine configurations.</li></ul><p><b>Service Tools and Training Available</b></p><p>The service tool kit has been updated to include parts that aid the installation of both existing and new crankshaft seals.</p><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. Reference Table 3.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 3, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>33224419</td><td>31 March 2021</td><td>Daventry</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on the engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb240007":"<h2>Camshaft Thrust Plate Installation</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QSK45 CM500 (All Versions)</li><li>QSK60 CM500 (All Versions)</li><li>QSK60 CM850 (All Versions)</li><li>QSK60 CM2150 (All Versions)</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 (All Versions)</li><li>QSK78 CM500 (All Versions)</li><li>QSK78 CM2350 (All Versions)</li></ul><p><b>Issue</b></p><p>Washer listed in Table 1 and Item B within Figure 1 <b>must</b> be used with the existing capscrews, and in the specified orientation, when installing new camshaft thrust plates.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01r00463.png\" alt=\"01r00463.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Factory Step 3 Exploded Diagram.</p><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><p>Factory step 3 action has been implemented to address under head embedment of the bolts in the camshaft thrust plate material. Installation of the washers should be targeted in a fix as fail situation or at a scheduled rebuild interval.</p><p><b>Service Instructions</b></p><ul><li>The washer orientation is critical.</li><li>There are two different faces to the washer; see Figure 2.</li><li>The burr face <b>must</b> be facing the camshaft thrust bearing plate during installation. Testing showed that incorrect orientation of the washer will result in a poor joint and embedment of a different form in the camshaft thrust plate material.</li></ul><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table A, Accompanies Figures 1 and 2</th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Item</td><td>Quantity</td><td>Additional Part Number</td><td>Description</td></tr><tr><td>A</td><td>4</td><td>EXISTING</td><td>CAPSCREW</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>4</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3176133\">3176133</a></td><td>WASHER</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>2</td><td>EXISTING</td><td>CAMSHAFT THRUST BEARING</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/01r00464.png\" alt=\"01r00464.png\"></figure><p>Figure 2, Detail AA Showing Correct Washer Orientation at Installation.</p><p><b>Service Parts Availability</b></p><p>Service parts are available. See Table 1 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>WASHER</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3176133\">3176133</a></td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 2.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>33234693</td><td>12 January 2024</td><td>Daventry</td></tr><tr><td>66307268</td><td>16 January 2024</td><td>Daventry</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on the engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb240023":"<h2>Fuel Shutoff Valve: External Fuel Leak</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QST30 CM552</li></ul><p><b>Issue Summary</b></p><p>Symptom:</p><ul><li>External fuel leak from fuel shutoff valve shield</li></ul><p>Root Cause:</p><ul><li>Fuel shutoff valve shield malfunction</li></ul><p><b>Resolution</b></p><p>The fuel shutoff valve is a serviceable component. This malfunction can be repaired by replacing the fuel shutoff valve shield. See QST30 Service Manual. Refer to Procedure 005-043 in Section 5 for replacement procedure.</p><p><b>Customer Communication</b></p><p>For customers experiencing frequent external fuel leaks from the fuel shutoff shield, customers should work with their local distributor or sales and services team to determine an appropriate interval to routinely replace both shutoff valve shields during planned downtime events.</p><p><b>Service Instructions</b></p><p>See QST30 Service Manual. Refer to Procedure 005-043 in Section 5 for instructions on fuel shutoff valve disassembly and assembly. Figure 1 below identifies the fuel shutoff valve shield in the overall assembly.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/05r00361.png\" alt=\"05r00361.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Exploded View of Fuel Shutoff Valve Assembly.</p><ol><li>Shutoff valve shield, Part Number 129839</li><li>Rectangular ring seal, Part Number 129888</li><li>Rectangular ring seal, Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4975411\">4975411</a></li></ol><p><b>Service Parts</b></p><p>See Table 1 for minimum part numbers that will be needed at time of Shutoff Valve Shield replacement.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Minimum Parts for Shutoff Valve Shield Maintenance Replacement (Per Engine)</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Part Number</td><td>Quantity</td></tr><tr><td>Shutoff Valve Shield</td><td>129839</td><td>2 (1 per fuel shutoff valve)</td></tr><tr><td>Rectangular Ring Seal</td><td>129888</td><td>2 (1 per fuel shutoff valve)</td></tr><tr><td>Rectangular Ring Seal</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4975411\">4975411</a></td><td>2 (1 per fuel shutoff valve)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb240059":"<h2>Repair Strategy for Fuel Lift Pump Relay and Wiring Harness: Fault Codes 583, 2265, 2266</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QST30 CM552</li></ul><p><b>Issue Summary</b></p><p>Symptom:</p><ul><li>Burned or malfunctioning fuel lift pump relay.</li><li>Engine Difficult to Start or Will Not Start (No Exhaust Smoke)</li><li>Potential fault codes:</li></ul><p><b>Verification</b></p><p>After following the published troubleshooting manual procedures, if a fuel lift pump relay is found to be dysfunctional, inspect the fuel lift pump relay and wiring harness connector for signs of heat damage, such as melted plastic.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/05r00365.png\" alt=\"05r00365.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Examples of Burned Fuel Lift Pump Relay and Wiring Harness Connector.</p><p><b>Resolution</b></p><p>If any signs of heat damage are present on either the fuel lift pump relay and/or the wiring harness connector, replace both the fuel lift pump relay and wiring harness.</p><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb240105":"<h2>New Pre-Lube Pressure Switch</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QST30 CM552</li><li>QST30 CM2350 T101</li><li>QST30 CM2350 T103C</li></ul><p><b>Issue Summary</b></p><p>Symptom:</p><ul><li>Engine will <b>not</b> start</li><li>Lubricating oil leaking through the pre-lube pressure switch</li></ul><p>Root Cause:</p><ul><li>Engine <b>not</b> starting- soldering the wires to the internal electrical contacts of the pre lube pressure switch.</li><li>Oil leaks- O-ring and diaphragm hardening because of unsuitable materials.</li></ul><p><b>Verification</b></p><ul><li>Visually inspect the pre-lube pressure switch to identify the lubricating oil leak through the vent hole on the switch.</li><li>Use multimeter to measure resistance of pre-lube switch when pressure is applied. Variable resistance or infinite resistance indicates poor wire connection.</li></ul><p><b>Resolution</b></p><ul><li>If customer is experiencing multiple pre-lube pressure switch malfunctions, replace malfunctioning switch with new pre-lube pressure switch.</li></ul><p><b>Service Instructions</b></p><ul><li>For pre lube pressure switch removal and installation instructions, see corresponding Service Manual. Reference Procedure 007-086 in Section 7.</li><li>Before connecting the pre-lube switch harness to the engine harness, use quick-dry electrical contact cleaner, Part Number 3824510, to remove all dirt and moisture from the pre-lube switch connector and the engine wiring harness connector.</li></ul><p><b>Service Parts</b></p><p>Service parts are offered. See Table 1 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Pre-lube Pressure Switch</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4924925\">4924925</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>6396482</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Inventory Action</b></p><p>Reallocate stock of the existing pre-lube pressure switch, Part Number <a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4924925\">4924925</a>, to QSK50 and QSK60 engines.</p><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 2.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Engine Model</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>37296673</td><td>QST30 CM552</td><td>7 April 2025</td><td>SEP</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb240108":"<h2>New Prelube Solenoid</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QSK60 CM500</li><li>QSK60 CM2250 K112</li><li>QSK60 CM2150 MCRS</li><li>QSK60 CM850 MCRS</li><li>QSK60 CM2150 MCRS G</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K116</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K117</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K136</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K121</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K135</li><li>QSK78 CM500</li><li>QSK78 CM2350 K126</li><li>QSK78 CM2350 K137</li><li>QSK78 CM2350 K154C</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><p>This document announces a new prelube solenoid, which requires related new OEM mounting bracketry and associated parts. The new bracketry ensures the correct orientation of the solenoid and offers the mounting arrangement required by the new prelube solenoid because of a change in the mounting dimensions.</p><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><p>A malfunction rate of the prelube solenoid has existed on multiple machines, stemming from vibration resulting in internal mechanical malfunction. To improve reliability, a new prelube solenoid and associated bracketry have been released to mitigate vibration and the resulting malfunction mechanism.</p><p><b>Service Parts</b></p><ul><li>New OEM mounting bracketry and associated parts are the responsibility of the OEM. See equipment manufacturer service information.</li><li>The new mounting bracketry and associated parts <b>must</b> be used when fitting the new prelube solenoid part number. See Table 1 for part number information.</li></ul><p>Service parts are offered. See Table 1 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Prelube Solenoid</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4951864\">4951864</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>5578249</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Compatibility</b></p><p>New OEM mounting bracketry and associated parts <b>must</b> be used when fitting the new prelube solenoid. See Table 1 for part number information.</p><p><b>Part Inventory Action</b></p><ul><li>QSK78 Products Affected: Use new prelube solenoid part number at point of TSB release.</li><li>QSK60 Products Affected: The new prelube solenoid part number is <b>not</b> used in production units. Use the new prelube solenoid part number as an approved countermeasure if the existing prelube solenoid part number has a high malfunction rate.</li><li>All Other Products: Continue to use existing prelube solenoid part number.</li></ul><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb240127":"<h2>New HE800 Turbocharger Bearing Housing Design</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QSK45 (All Versions)</li><li>QSK50 (All Versions)</li><li>QSK60 (All Versions)</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><p>New HE800 turbochargers are being released with a new design of bearing housing which contains a coolant jacket for coolant cooling. This bearing housing has been designed so that the turbocharger can be used both wet and dry depending on if the engine has coolant plumbing already built in.</p><p><b>Service Instructions</b></p><p>If the turbocharger is going to be run dry, there will be no risk made by debris getting into the turbocharger as the coolant channel is completely sealed off from the turbocharger. Any debris that will enter the turbocharger will sit in a coolant channel. Do <b>not</b> block off any coolant ports as this could cause a heat trap and result in an overheated turbocharger.</p><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb240152":"<h2>New Engine Heat Shields</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QSK60 CM2150 K132M</li><li>QSK60 CM2150 MCRS</li><li>QSK60 CM2150 MCRS M</li><li>QSK60 M2150 MCRS G</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><p>A new heat shield design has been released for the single stage variant, which introduces an improved clearance for other components such as injector fuel supply line, low temperature aftercooling pipe, and turbocharger oil drain.</p><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><ul><li>This change has come out of product improvement where some clearance issues were seen to cause malfunctions of different components (injector fuel supply line, low temperature aftercooling pipe, and turbocharger oil drain). The design was improved to reduce future malfunctions.</li><li>Because of the complexity of the change, see the below graphics to understand the change in geometry for each option.</li></ul><p>Option SH6726:</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/17r02218.png\" alt=\"17r02218.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Option SH6726 With Service Covers.</p><p>Option SH6733:</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/17r02219.png\" alt=\"17r02219.png\"></figure><p>Figure 2, Option SH6733 with Service Covers.</p><p>Option SH6711:</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/17r02220.png\" alt=\"17r02220.png\"></figure><p>Figure 3, Option SH6711 with Service Covers.</p><p>Option SH6717:</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/17r02221.png\" alt=\"17r02221.png\"></figure><p>Figure 4, Option SH6717 with Service Covers.</p><p>Parts Changing:</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/17r02222.png\" alt=\"17r02222.png\"></figure><p>Figure 5, New Parts Being Released.</p><p>The parts highlighted in green and yellow in Figure 5 are the new parts and are contained within all option graphics listed above.</p><p><b>Service Parts Availability</b></p><p>Service parts are available. See Table 1, 2 and 3 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, SH6717 Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Hexagon Flange Head Cap Screw</td><td>3643856</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>5538544</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>3467982</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6380568</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>3467963</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6380570</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>3649494</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6380571</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>3649504</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6380572</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield Bracket</td><td>3649500</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6380573</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield Bracket</td><td>3644722</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6380574</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield Bracket</td><td>3649501</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6380575</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield Bracket</td><td>3649503</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6380576</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>3644740</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6458606</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>3644743</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6458607</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>3467981</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6324636</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>3467967</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6324639</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>3467968</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6449431</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>3467972</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6324644</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>3646154</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6324647</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>3467985</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6380755</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>3467971</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6383803</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, SH6726 Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Hexagon Flange Head Cap Screw</td><td>3643856</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>5538544</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>3467963</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6380570</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>3649494</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6380571</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>3649504</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6380572</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield Bracket</td><td>3649500</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6380573</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield Bracket</td><td>3644722</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6380574</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield Bracket</td><td>3649501</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6380575</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield Bracket</td><td>3649503</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6380576</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>5538016</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6445931</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>3644740</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6458606</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>3644743</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6458607</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>3467981</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6324636</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>3467967</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6324639</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>3467968</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6449431</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>3467972</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6324644</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>3646154</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6324647</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>3467971</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6383803</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>5540877</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6457297</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 3, SH6733 Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Hexagon Flange Head Cap Screw</td><td>3643856</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>5538544</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>3467963</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6380570</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>3649494</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6380571</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>3649504</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6380572</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield Bracket</td><td>3649500</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6380573</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield Bracket</td><td>3644722</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6380574</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield Bracket</td><td>3649501</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6380575</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield Bracket</td><td>3649503</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6380576</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>5538016</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6445931</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>3644740</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6458606</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>3644743</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6458607</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>3467981</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6324636</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>3467967</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6324639</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>3467968</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6449431</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>3467972</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6324644</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>3467996</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6324645</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>3646154</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6324647</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>5538019</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6457296</td></tr><tr><td>Heat Shield</td><td>3467982</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>6445931</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Identification</b></p><p>Parts are engraved with part numbers.</p><p><b>Part Inventory Action</b></p><p>Use existing part number inventory before using the new part number.</p><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 2.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>33235792</td><td>13 May 2024</td><td>Daventry Engine Plant</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on the engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb240153":"<h2>New Turbocharger Compressor Wheel for Single Stage HE800 Mining Engines: Turbocharger Compressor Wheel Splitting</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QSK50 CM2150 MCRS</li></ul><p><b>Issue</b></p><p>Aluminium HE800 Turbocharger compressor wheels used in single stage mining applications have malfunctioned because of fatigue. The compressor wheel splits through the wheel hub centre line and damages the compressor cover, turbine wheel and shaft. Malfunctions can spread debris into the aftercoolers on the affected bank.</p><p>Symptom:</p><ul><li>Reports of low power and black smoke from the affected bank</li><li>Turbocharger compressor wheel is found split through the hub center line</li></ul><p>Root Cause:</p><ul><li>The existing turbocharger compressor wheel material can experience crack initiation and fatigue propagation under certain duty cycle loads.</li></ul><p><b>Verification</b></p><ul><li>Verify low power, black smoke from the affected bank, and low intake manifold pressure.</li><li>Turbocharger compressor inspection is required to verify the failure mode. The turbocharger compressor wheel will be split in two or three pieces (Figure 1).</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/17r02223.png\" alt=\"17r02223.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Turbocharger Compressor Wheel Split Malfunction.</p><p><b>Resolution</b></p><ul><li>Turbochargers <b>must</b> be replaced with new parts when the split compressor wheel failure mode has been verified. Remove aftercooler covers and clean or replace aftercoolers which are damaged or contaminated with compressor wheel debris.</li><li>In the event of a turbocharger malfunction, the use of the new turbocharger part number is recommended. See Table 1.</li><li>A new compressor wheel material has been used to improve HE800 robustness in single stage turbocharged mine haul truck and excavator applications. New turbocharger part numbers have been released for production and service. New turbochargers retain every feature of the previous part numbers, aside from the improved compressor wheel material.</li><li>The new compressor wheel material has improved capability to resist increased stress at the compressor wheel to oil slinger interface. Increased stresses have been attributed to turbocharger resonance under certain running conditions.</li></ul><p><b>Service Instructions</b></p><ul><li>If a turbocharger malfunction has been verified, remove aftercooler covers to inspect for debris. Clean the aftercooler housing and core if required. Install the aftercooler and covers. See QSK50 CM2150 MCRS Service Manual, Bulletin <a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/4021528\">4021528</a>. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-010-008-tr\">Refer to Procedure 010-008</a> in Section 10.</li><li>Replace turbocharger. See QSK50 CM2150 MCRS Service Manual, Bulletin <a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/4021528\">4021528</a>. <a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/28-010-033-tr\">Refer to Procedure 010-033</a> in Section 19.</li></ul><p><b>Service Parts Availability</b></p><ul><li>Service parts are available. See Table 1 for part numbers.</li><li>Turbocharger availability for service will be in the form turbocharger kits <b>only</b>. The kit will include the new turbocharger, as well as the required gaskets and seals to replace one turbocharger.</li></ul><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>KIT, TURBOCHARGER (Left Bank)</td><td>4956149</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>6524218</td></tr><tr><td>KIT, TURBOCHARGER (Right Bank)</td><td>4956148</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>6524187</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Identification</b></p><p>Turbocharger dataplates mounted to the compressor cover display part number details. For production units, <b>not</b> previously replaced, customer numbers (shown as “Cust” on the dataplate) can be used to identify turbocharger kit part numbers. For previously replaced turbochargers, a turbocharger kit part number will be shown on the turbocharger dataplate. See Table 2 for details.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Customer Number to Kit Part Number Guide</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td></td><td>Dataplate Customer Number</td><td>Related Turbocharger Kit</td></tr><tr><td>Existing Parts</td><td>4041284</td><td>4956149 (Left Bank)</td></tr><tr><td>4041286</td><td>4956148 (Right Bank)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>New Parts</td><td>6524217</td><td>6524218 (Left Bank)</td></tr><tr><td>6524129</td><td>6524187 (Right Bank)</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Both new part numbers are implemented for production and were built into the ESN First. See Table 3.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 3, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>33236236</td><td>8 July 2024</td><td>Daventry Engine Plant</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb240157":"<h2>Valve Traceability for ReCon® Cylinder Heads</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QSK45 CM500</li><li>QSK60 CM2150 MCRS</li><li>QSK60 CM2150 MCRS G</li><li>QSK60 CM2150 MCRS M</li><li>QSK60 CM2250 K112</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K116</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K117</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K121</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K135</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K136</li><li>QSK60 CM500</li><li>QSK60 CM850 MCRS</li></ul><p><b>Customer Communication</b></p><p>Traceability of valves ReCon® Cylinder heads is found on stem of valve as shown in Figures 1 and 2.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/02r00175.png\" alt=\"02r00175.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Valve Stem with Traceability Code.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/02r00176.png\" alt=\"02r00176.png\"></figure><p>Figure 2, Valve Stem with Traceability Code.</p><p>If no traceability code exists on the valve stem, reference the cylinder head serial number as shown in Figures 3 and 4 below.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/02r00177.png\" alt=\"02r00177.png\"></figure><p>Figure 3, Cylinder Head Serial Number and Part Number Location.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/02r00178.png\" alt=\"02r00178.png\"></figure><p>Figure 4, Cylinder Head Serial Number and Part Number.</p><p>Contact Cummins Care™ and provide the cylinder head serial number to get more details regarding the valves.</p><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb240188":"<h2>New Electric Fuel Lift Pump System</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QST30 CM552</li><li>QST30 CM850</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><p>A new electric fuel lift pump system has been released for the product affected. This change includes a new supplier for the fuel lift pumps, an increase from two to three fuel lift pumps per engine, and new relevant hardware. See Table 3. These new fuel lift pumps are added to the Operation and Maintenance Manual, Bulletin <a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/3666134\">3666134</a>, as maintenance items.</p><p><b>Service Instructions</b></p><p>Select the Lift Pump Base Service Kit, Filter Bracket, and Service Wiring Harness from Table 1 needed for the relevant application and configuration. See corresponding Service Manual. Reference Procedure 005-045 in Section 5, for removal, clean and inspect, and install instructions for the fuel lift pumps. Reference procedure 006-017 in section 6 of the corresponding service manual for removal, clean and inspect and install instructions for the fuel filter head. For new conversions, reference procedure 006-017 in Section 6 of the corresponding service manual for the removal of the old hardware. Reference both procedures 005-045 and 006-017 in Sections 5 and 6 of the corresponding service manual for the installation of the new hardware.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Kits/Parts Needed for Conversion from Two-Pump to Three-Pump System</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Application/Configuration</td><td>Description</td><td>Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Base Kit</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>All</td><td>Fuel Lift Pump Base Service Kit</td><td>6563609</td></tr><tr><td>Filter Bracket</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Engines built with one of the options: FF 5079 FF 5080 FF 5086 FF 5088 FF 5105 FF 5107 FF 5108</td><td>Filter Bracket (See Figure 1 in Part Identification section)</td><td>6426058</td></tr><tr><td>Engines built with one of the options: FF 5081 FF 5106</td><td>Filter Bracket with Air Plumbing Support (See Figure 2 in Part Identification section)</td><td>6426729</td></tr><tr><td>Service Wiring Harness</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>PowerGen (12V System)</td><td>Wiring Harness (2 pump to 3 pump – PG 12V to 24V)</td><td>6470469</td></tr><tr><td>PowerGen (24V System)</td><td>Wiring Harness (2 pump to 3 pump – PG 24V)</td><td>6470470</td></tr><tr><td>Industrial (12V or 24V System)</td><td>Wiring Harness (2 pump to 3 pump – IND)</td><td>6474493</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Kits Needed for <b>Fuel Pump Head Reassembly (No Malfunctioning Fuel Lift Pumps)</b></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Description</td><td>Part Number</td><td>Quantity</td></tr><tr><td>Lift Pump Consumables Kit (3 sets of isolators and seals)</td><td>6563611</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 3, Kit Needed for Routine Maintenance and Malfunction Replacements</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Description</td><td>Part Number</td><td>Quantity</td></tr><tr><td>Lift Pump Replacement Kit (Consumables Kit + 3 Fuel Lift Pumps)</td><td>6563610</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>For field repairs, all three fuel lift pumps <b>must</b> be replaced if one or more fuel lift pump malfunctions.</p><p><b>Service Parts</b></p><p>Service parts are offered. See Table 4 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 4, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Fuel Lift Pump</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5483862\">5483862</a></td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>6413638</td></tr><tr><td>Fuel Filter Head</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5483701\">5483701</a></td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>6425066</td></tr><tr><td>Fuel Pump Head</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5483705\">5483705</a></td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>6425068</td></tr><tr><td>Vibration Isolator</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4067902\">4067902</a></td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>6425064</td></tr><tr><td>Filter Bracket</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/4295451\">4295451</a></td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>6426058</td></tr><tr><td>Filter Bracket with Air Plumbing Support</td><td>4975740</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>6426729</td></tr><tr><td>Wiring Harness (2 pump to 3 pump – PowerGen 12V to 24V)</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>6470469</td></tr><tr><td>Wiring Harness (2 pump to 3 pump – PowerGen 24V)</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>6470470</td></tr><tr><td>Wiring Harness (2 pump to 3 pump – Industrial)</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>6474493</td></tr><tr><td>Fuel Pump Kit</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5593523\">5593523</a></td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>See Table 1</td></tr><tr><td>Lift Pump Base Conversion Kit</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>6563609</td></tr><tr><td>Lift Pump Consumables Kit (3 sets of isolators and seals)</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>6563611</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Inventory Action</b></p><p>Use existing part number inventory before using the new part number.</p><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 5.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 5, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Application</td><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>PowerGen</td><td>37295184</td><td>4 October 2024</td><td>Seymour Engine Plant</td></tr><tr><td>Industrial</td><td>37295256</td><td>14 October 2024</td><td>Seymour Engine Plant</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on the engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Publications Affected</b></p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th><b>Table 5, Publications Affected</b></th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Manual Type</b></td><td><b>Engine</b></td><td><b>Bulletin Number</b></td><td><b>Procedure Title</b></td><td><b>Procedure</b></td><td><b>Section</b></td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QST30</td><td><a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/4021539\">4021539</a></td><td>Fuel Lift Pump</td><td>Refer to Procedure 005-045</td><td>5</td></tr><tr><td>Operations and Maintenance Manual</td><td>QST30</td><td><a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/3666134\">3666134</a></td><td>Maintenance Schedule</td><td><a class=\"lnk doc\" href=\"#/doc/57-102-002-om\">Refer to Procedure 102-002</a></td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Operations and Maintenance Manual</td><td>QST30</td><td><a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/3666134\">3666134</a></td><td>Fuel Lift Pump</td><td>Refer to Procedure 005-045</td><td>6</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Identification</b></p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/10r00495.png\" alt=\"10r00495.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Filter Bracket, Part Number 6426058.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/10r00496.png\" alt=\"10r00496.png\"></figure><p>Figure 2, Filter Bracket with Air Plumbing Support, Part Number 6426729.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/05m00182.png\" alt=\"05m00182.png\"></figure><p>Figure 3, Comparison of Installed Two-Pump (Left) and Three-Pump (Right) Systems.</p><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb250016":"<h2>Engine Control Module (ECM) Calibration Code Change to Exhaust Gas Temperature (EGT) Sensor Thresholds: White Smoke</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QSK60 CM2350 K116</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K121</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K135</li><li>QSK60 CM2350 K136</li></ul><p><b>Issue</b></p><p>Exhaust gas temperature (EGT) thresholds for setting fault codes in the engine control module (ECM) are too high to alert operators to early exhaust valve cracking and subsequent malfunction.</p><p>Symptom:</p><ul><li>White smoke from the exhaust and an underperforming cylinder at the affected position</li></ul><p>Root Cause:</p><ul><li>EGT threshold values in the ECM calibration are set too high and do <b>not</b> illuminate warning lamps early enough to alert operators to valve malfunction.</li></ul><p><b>Resolution</b></p><p>The ECM calibration codes have been changed to lower the EGT thresholds.</p><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><p>ECM calibration codes have been changed to lower the EGT thresholds from existing values. All products affected by this TSB share the same change. See Table 1.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, EGT Threshold Changes</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fault Code Threshold</td><td>Existing EGT Value °C [ °F ]</td><td>New EGT Value °C [ °F ]</td></tr><tr><td>Moderately Severe Level (Amber Lamp)</td><td>732°C [ 1349°F ]</td><td>615 °C [ 1139°F ]</td></tr><tr><td>Most Severe Level (Red Lamp)</td><td>-</td><td>630 °C [ 1166°F ]</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><p>High exhaust gas temperature fault codes will alert operators of potential exhaust valve malfunction to mitigate further engine damage.</p><p><b>Service Instructions</b></p><ul><li>For CM2350 engines, the new ECM calibration code is released for child 2 ECM. See Table 2.</li></ul><p>New ECM calibration codes are available as below:</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, QSK60 T4 ECM Calibration Codes</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>QSK60 CM2350 ECM Codes (Child 2)</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>CX60029.05</td><td>CX60149.05</td><td>CX60062.11</td></tr><tr><td>CX60050.13</td><td>CX60170.02</td><td>CX60080.11</td></tr><tr><td>CX60053.15</td><td>CX60173.02</td><td>CX60083.11</td></tr><tr><td>CX60056.15</td><td>CX60176.02</td><td>CX60095.10</td></tr><tr><td>CX60065.11</td><td>CX60179.02</td><td>CX60098.10</td></tr><tr><td>CX60071.12</td><td>CX60182.02</td><td>CX60116.11</td></tr><tr><td>CX60074.11</td><td>CX60191.02</td><td>CX60140.05</td></tr><tr><td>CX60077.11</td><td>CX60193.02</td><td>CX60146.05</td></tr><tr><td>CX60137.05</td><td>CX60217.02</td><td>CX60205.02</td></tr><tr><td>CX60143.05</td><td>CX60059.11</td><td>CX60208.02</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Compatibility</b></p><p>These ECM calibrations are cross compatible with existing parent and child calibrations.</p><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Originally implemented for production on Tier 2 and 4 products. See Table 3.</p><p>Tier 2 products are no longer built with this change; therefore, the last engine build information is displayed in Table 4.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 3, ESN First Information</th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Engine Service Models</td><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>QSK60 CM2150 MCRS</td><td>33237928</td><td>6 January 2025</td><td>Daventry Engine Plant</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on the engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 4, Tier 2 Engines Information</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Engine Service Models</td><td>Last Build Date 1</td></tr><tr><td>QSK60 CM2150 MCRS QSK60 CM850 MCRS</td><td>3 September 2025</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on the engine dataplate.</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb250017":"<h2>New Accessory Drive Seal: Engine Lubricating Oil Leak</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>KT38</li><li>KTA38</li><li>KTA50</li><li>KTTA38</li><li>KTTA50</li><li>QSK38 CM2150 K106</li><li>QSK38 CM2150 MCRS</li><li>QSK38 CM2350 K125</li><li>QSK38 CM2350 K138M</li><li>QSK38 CM850 MCRS</li><li>QSK50 CM2150 K107</li><li>QSK50 CM2150 MCRS</li><li>QSK50 CM2350 K108</li><li>QSK50 CM2350 K130</li><li>QSK50 CM500</li><li>QSK50 CM850 MCRS</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><ul><li>A new accessory drive seal with a revised design has been released.</li><li>The new accessory drive seal uses a single piece construction.</li><li>To install the new accessory drive seal, a new service tool has been released.</li></ul><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><p>The existing accessory drive seal can leak engine lubricating oil.</p><p><b>Service Instructions</b></p><ul><li>All existing pulley part numbers have been obsoleted and superseded by new pulley part numbers.</li><li>New pulleys are sold without a wear sleeve fitted to the pulley shaft, allowing the use of the new accessory drive seal.</li><li>A new pulley <b>must</b> be used when installing the new accessory drive seal. A new accessory drive seal installer part number is available and listed in Table 1.</li><li><b>Never</b> remove the wear sleeve from existing pulleys because of the risk of introducing an engine lubricating oil leak path.</li><li>New pulley part numbers are shown in Table 1, allowing cross reference from the existing pulley part numbers.</li></ul><p><b>Service Parts Availability</b></p><ul><li>Service parts are available. See Table 1 for part numbers.</li><li>Existing part numbers will remain available for service on the KV19 and QSK19 engines <b>only</b>.</li></ul><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>ACCESSORY DRIVE SEAL</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3628895\">3628895</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5694571\">5694571</a></td></tr><tr><td>ACCESSORY DRIVE SEAL</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3628894\">3628894</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5694571\">5694571</a></td></tr><tr><td>ACCESSORY DRIVE SEAL INSTALLER</td><td>-</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>5573466</td></tr><tr><td>PULLEY, ACCESSORY DRIVE</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3015546\">3015546</a></td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3628938\">3628938</a></td></tr><tr><td>PULLEY, ACCESSORY DRIVE</td><td>3018761</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3015321\">3015321</a></td></tr><tr><td>PULLEY, ACCESSORY DRIVE</td><td>3028587</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>3177006</td></tr><tr><td>PULLEY, ACCESSORY DRIVE</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3045970\">3045970</a></td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3628937\">3628937</a></td></tr><tr><td>PULLEY, ACCESSORY DRIVE</td><td>3179943</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>3628940</td></tr><tr><td>PULLEY, ACCESSORY DRIVE</td><td>3179944</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>3628939</td></tr><tr><td>PULLEY, ACCESSORY DRIVE</td><td>3631843</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>3631844</td></tr><tr><td>PULLEY, ACCESSORY DRIVE</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3645612\">3645612</a></td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3645579\">3645579</a></td></tr><tr><td>PULLEY, ALTERNATOR</td><td>3880348</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>3875798</td></tr><tr><td>PULLEY, ACCESSORY DRIVE</td><td>5538240</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>5538239</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Service Tools and Training Available</b></p><p>A new accessory drive seal installer part number is available and listed in Table 1.</p><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 2.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>33227622</td><td>21 February 2022</td><td>Daventry</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on the engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Publications Affected</b></p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 3, Publications Affected</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Manual Type</td><td>Engine</td><td>Bulletin Number</td><td>Procedure Title</td><td>Procedure</td><td>Section</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QSK38/50</td><td><a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/4021528\">4021528</a></td><td>Accessory Drive Seal</td><td>Refer to Procedure 001-003</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QSK38 CM2150 K106</td><td>4332768</td><td>Accessory Drive Seal</td><td>Refer to Procedure 001-003</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QSK50 CM2150 K107</td><td>4332773</td><td>Accessory Drive Seal</td><td>Refer to Procedure 001-003</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QSK50 CM2350 K108</td><td>4332823</td><td>Accessory Drive Seal</td><td>Refer to Procedure 001-003</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QSK50 CM2350 K130</td><td>5504180</td><td>Accessory Drive Seal</td><td>Refer to Procedure 001-003</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QSK50 CM2350 K130</td><td>5504180</td><td>Gear Cover, Front</td><td>Refer to Procedure 001-031</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QSK38 CM2350 K138M</td><td>5613395</td><td>Accessory Drive Seal</td><td>Refer to Procedure 001-003</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QSK38 CM2350 K138M</td><td>5613395</td><td>Gear Cover, Front</td><td>Refer to Procedure 001-031</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb250112":"<h2>Lubricating Oil Leaks from Turbocharger Compressor Outlet</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QST30 CM552</li></ul><p><b>Issue Summary</b></p><p>Symptom:</p><ul><li>External lubricating oil leak from turbocharger compressor outlet and/or air crossover inlet</li></ul><p>Root Cause:</p><ul><li>With prolonged operation in low-load and low-speed conditions, lubricating oil can accumulate in the turbocharger bearing housing and leak past the turbocharger compressor seal, leaking externally at the air crossover hose connection between the turbocharger compressor outlet and air crossover inlet.</li></ul><p><b>Verification</b></p><p>Visually inspect the turbocharger compressor outlet, air crossover inlet and air crossover hose for signs of an external lubricating oil leak.</p><p><b>Resolution</b></p><p>Fast-setting RTV is to be applied to the hose connections at the turbocharger compressor outlet and air crossover inlet. Reference Service Instruction section.</p><p><b>Service Instruction</b></p><p>Reference the steps below for the bank(s) leaking lubricating oil:</p><ul><li>Loosen the T bolt clamps (1) on the air crossover hose (2).</li><li>Refer to Procedure 010-019 in Section 10 to remove the air crossover.</li><li>Remove the T bolt clamps (1) from the air crossover hose (2) and remove the hose from the turbocharger compressor outlet (4).</li><li>Clean the outer surfaces of the air crossover inlet (3) and turbocharger compressor outlet (4).</li><li>Clean the inner surface of the air crossover hose (2).</li><li>Apply a bead of Loctite RTV Gasket Maker: SI 587, Part Number 135504, or equivalent, to the outer surface of the air crossover inlet (3) and the turbocharger compressor outlet (4) behind the lip of the inlet/outlet.</li><li>Install the air crossover hose (2) onto the turbocharger compressor outlet (4).</li><li>Loosely install the T bolt clamps (1) onto the hose (2).</li><li>Refer to Procedure 010-019 in Section 10 to install the air crossover (3).</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/10r00512.png\" alt=\"10r00512.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Example of Turbocharger Joint (LB shown).</p><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb250118":"<h2>Obsoleting the Re-use Criteria for Crankshaft Adapter Capscrews</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>K50</li><li>QSK50 CM850 MCRS</li><li>QSK50 CM2150 MCRS</li><li>QSK50 CM2350 K108</li><li>QSK50 CM2350 K130</li></ul><p><b>Issue Summary</b></p><p>Symptom:</p><ul><li>Crankshaft adapter capscrews have been found broken upon inspection and during engine rebuilds.</li></ul><p><b>Resolution</b></p><ul><li><b>Always</b> replace crankshaft adapter capscrews during rebuilds and mid-life events.</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/ks6adma.png\" alt=\"ks6adma.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Crankshaft Adapter Capscrews.</p><p><b>Publications Affected</b></p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Publications Affected</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Manual Type</td><td>Engine</td><td>Bulletin Number</td><td>Procedure Title</td><td>Procedure</td><td>Section</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>K50</td><td><a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/4021528\">4021528</a></td><td>Crankshaft Adapter</td><td>Refer to Procedure 001-017</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QSK50 CM850 MCRS</td><td><a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/4021528\">4021528</a></td><td>Crankshaft Adapter</td><td>Refer to Procedure 001-017</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QSK50 CM2150 MCRS</td><td><a class=\"lnk man\" href=\"#/manual/4021528\">4021528</a></td><td>Crankshaft Adapter</td><td>Refer to Procedure 001-017</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QSK50 CM2350 K108</td><td>4332823</td><td>Crankshaft Adapter</td><td>Refer to Procedure 001-017</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Service Manual</td><td>QSK50 CM2350 K130</td><td>5504180</td><td>Crankshaft Adapter</td><td>Refer to Procedure 001-017</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb250124":"<h2>Hybrid MCRS Fuel Pump Service Kit Upfit</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QSK60 CM2150 MCRS</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><ul><li>A new fuel pump and associated hardware has been released to improve reliability of the pumping element plunger.</li></ul><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><ul><li>The new fuel pump design has improved fuel flow routing to reduce fuel temperature inside the fuel pump when frequently operating under low load.</li><li>A new high pressure fuel line has been released to allow the new fuel pump to be fitted on two stage turbocharged engines.</li><li>A service kit has been released, containing additional hardware required to upfit an existing engine with the new fuel pump and high-pressure fuel line.</li></ul><p><b>Service Instructions</b></p><ul><li>Service parts are available. See Table 1 and Table 2. Part numbers vary depending on engine model (single stage or two stage).</li></ul><p><b>Service Parts Availability</b></p><ul><li>Service parts are available. See Table 1 and Table 2.</li><li>Part numbers vary depending on engine model (single stage or two stage).</li></ul><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts - QSK60 CM2150 MCRS Single Stage</th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Fuel pump</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>6472212</td></tr><tr><td>Service Kit</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>6513807</td></tr><tr><td>High Pressure Fuel Line (LB)</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>5592789</td></tr><tr><td>High Pressure Fuel Line (RB)</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>5592785</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Service Parts - QSK60 CM2150 MCRS Two Stage</th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Fuel pump</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>6472212</td></tr><tr><td>Service Kit</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>6513807</td></tr><tr><td>High Pressure Fuel Line (LB)</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>5592789</td></tr><tr><td>High Pressure Fuel Line (RB)</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>6556952</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb250125":"<h2>New Engine Wiring Harnesses</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QST30 CM552</li><li>QST30 CM850</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><ul><li>New engine wiring harnesses are released in correspondence with the new 3-pump electric fuel lift pump system.</li><li>There are now three electric fuel lift pump power connections on the engine wiring harnesses. The branch of the engine wiring harness that includes the fuel lift pump relay has also been extended in length to achieve a more accessible location when installed on-engine and increased in wire gauge to the appropriate size for the new system. Depending on the mounting location of the fuel lift pump and filter head assembly, the fuel lift pump harness assembly may be routed in one of two paths, as shown in Figures 1 and 2. The wiring diagrams have been updated for all possible engine wiring harness options.</li></ul><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/00r01909.png\" alt=\"00r01909.png\"></figure><p>Figure 1, Engine Left Bank Fuel Lift Pump Relay Location.</p><figure class=\"fig\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/figures/00r01910.png\" alt=\"00r01910.png\"></figure><p>Figure 2, Engine Rear Fuel Lift Pump Relay Location.</p><p><b>Service Parts</b></p><p>Service parts are offered. See Table 1 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>Industrial Wiring Harness (Lift Pump Harness Assembly)</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5544940\">5544940</a></td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>6426736</td></tr><tr><td>Industrial Wiring Harness (Main Engine Harness Assembly)</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5483706\">5483706</a></td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>6426740</td></tr><tr><td>G-Drive Wiring Harness (Main Engine Harness Assembly)</td><td>5544939</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>6426743</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Inventory Action</b></p><p>Use existing part number inventory before using the new part number.</p><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 2.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Harness Part Number</td><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>6426743</td><td>37296947</td><td>5 May 2025</td><td>Seymour Engine Plant</td></tr><tr><td>6426736</td><td>37297094</td><td>27 May 2025</td><td>Seymour Engine Plant</td></tr><tr><td>6426740</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on the engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>","tsb250131":"<h2>Steel Edge Bonded Gear Housing Gasket</h2><h3>Warranty Statement</h3><p>The information in this document has no effect on present warranty coverage or repair practices, nor does it authorize TRP or Campaign actions.</p><h3>Contents</h3><p><b>Product Affected</b></p><ul><li>QSK38 CM2150 K106</li><li>QSK38 CM2150 K142M</li><li>QSK38 CM2350 K125</li><li>QSK38 CM2350 K138M</li><li>QSK38 CM2350 K145G</li><li>QSK50 CM2150 K107</li><li>QSK50 CM2150 K140M</li><li>QSK50 CM2350 K108</li><li>QSK50 CM2350 K130</li><li>QSK50 CM2350 K146G</li></ul><p><b>Description of Change</b></p><ul><li>A steel edge bonded rubber gasket version of the existing gear housing fiber gasket released and added as an optional addition.</li><li>The sealing of the two different gaskets differs significantly therefore the change in gasket material has also driven dimensional changes to the gear housing, gear cover and camshaft cover (gear cover) designs. A shim has also been released to ensure the air gap for the camshaft position sensor is maintained. A procedure currently exists in the service manuals to allow for shimming of the camshaft position sensor without the need for the specific OEM shim.</li></ul><p><b>Reason for Change</b></p><p>Improved lubrication oil sealing between the gear cover and housing and the cylinder block.</p><p><b>Customer Communication</b></p><p>The additional gear housing gaskets are a steel edge bonded rubber gasket and have been released alongside the existing fiber gear housing gasket which will continue to be available as a production and service item for existing engines.</p><p><b>Service Instructions</b></p><p>Table 1 shows where the camshaft position sensor needs to be shimmed. Should an OEM shim <b>not</b> be available, a procedure does exist to machine a shim locally to meet specific requirements. See corresponding Service Manual. Reference Procedure 019-363 in Section 19.</p><p><b>Service Parts Availability</b></p><p>Service parts are available. See Table 1 for part numbers.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 1, Service Parts</th><th></th><th></th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part Description</td><td>Existing Part Number</td><td>Obsolete</td><td>Superseded</td><td>New Part Number</td></tr><tr><td>GASKET</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3644502\">3644502</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5539807\">5539807</a></td></tr><tr><td>COVER, GEAR (Without Camshaft Cover)</td><td>3638995</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>6389444​</td></tr><tr><td>COVER, GEAR (With Camshaft Cover)</td><td>5694541</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>6389476*</td></tr><tr><td>HOUSING, GEAR</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/3176918\">3176918</a></td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><a class=\"lnk part\" href=\"#/part/5374581\">5374581</a></td></tr><tr><td>COVER, CAMSHAFT</td><td>5374477​</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>5539411*</td></tr><tr><td>SHIM</td><td>-</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>6398296</td></tr><tr><td>*Shim Required When Using New Part Number</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Part Compatibility</b></p><ul><li>The new steel edge bonded gaskets is <b>not</b> to be used with the fiber gasket gear covers and housings. Because of the increased thickness of the steel edge bonded gasket and narrower sealing patterns, contact interactions with the gear train and electronics exist.</li><li>When upgrading engines originally fitted with fiber gear housing gaskets to the new steel edge bonded gear housing gaskets the new gear cover and housing part numbers in Table 1 <b>must</b> be used to prevent damage to the gear train and electrical components.</li></ul><p><b>Part Identification</b></p><ul><li>New part is stainless-steel and is easily recognizable compared to the fiber gasket.</li><li>The part number permanently applied on the steel carrier of the new gaskets.</li></ul><p><b>Production Status</b></p><p>Implemented for production. See Table 2.</p><div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"doc-table\"><thead><tr><th>Table 2, Production Information</th><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ESN First</td><td>Build Date 1</td><td>Plant</td></tr><tr><td>33239155</td><td>26 May 2025</td><td>Daventry</td></tr><tr><td>1 Engine build date can be found on the engine dataplate.</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Document History</h3>"};
